131
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:53,870
All right.

132
00:06:53,870 --> 00:06:55,810
So to start, I'll give
a very brief overview

133
00:06:55,810 --> 00:06:57,730
of transformers, what they are.

134
00:06:57,730 --> 00:07:01,130
It's the title of the course, of
course, and also the direction

135
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:04,290
that the field has taken
to get up to transformers.

136
00:07:04,290 --> 00:07:08,130
So early ML around
pre-2012, most work

137
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:12,090
consisted of some of
hand-engineered features

138
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:14,930
relevant to the task at
hand, maybe classification

139
00:07:14,930 --> 00:07:16,570
or some other thing.

140
00:07:16,570 --> 00:07:19,770
These were passed into
shallow models which

141
00:07:19,770 --> 00:07:21,450
then were trained
to make predictions

142
00:07:21,450 --> 00:07:23,890
based on these features.

143
00:07:23,890 --> 00:07:25,990
And in order to
train your models,

144
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:30,250
you had a generally smallish
set of expensive labels that

145
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:33,370
were collected by hand that you
compared the model's predictions

146
00:07:33,370 --> 00:07:35,850
to make a feedback
loop that allowed

147
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:38,650
you to update the parameters.

148
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:41,930
Then we gradually had a move
to supervise deep learning,

149
00:07:41,930 --> 00:07:45,290
so replacing the hand-engineered
features as the models

150
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:48,530
got bigger with raw
data, so the data

151
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:51,083
that was used to
generate the features.

152
00:07:51,083 --> 00:07:53,250
And in this way, you went
directly from the raw data

153
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:55,810
to the predictions,
taking out that middleman

154
00:07:55,810 --> 00:08:00,050
step of hand engineering.

155
00:08:00,050 --> 00:08:02,630
Going further, we move to
self-supervised learning.

156
00:08:02,630 --> 00:08:06,170
So here, we thought even
having these expensive labels

157
00:08:06,170 --> 00:08:07,690
was too much.

158
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:10,770
And so models were
trained here by corrupting

159
00:08:10,770 --> 00:08:13,290
the raw data in some way,
maybe noising, or masking,

160
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:15,850
or that sort of thing, and
then training the model

161
00:08:15,850 --> 00:08:19,130
to reconstruct the
original uncorrupted data.

162
00:08:19,130 --> 00:08:22,090
And from this, you learned very
general raw representations

163
00:08:22,090 --> 00:08:24,690
that could be applied to a
variety of downstream tasks.

164
00:08:24,690 --> 00:08:27,050
So after this, you could
use your model and fine

165
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:31,410
tune it to do whatever
task you wanted it to.

166
00:08:31,410 --> 00:08:34,330
As a case study of language,
a lot of early work

167
00:08:34,330 --> 00:08:38,549
was like sentiment analysis, so
taking in short pieces of text,

168
00:08:38,549 --> 00:08:41,250
say, reviews, and just trying
to classify whether they're

169
00:08:41,250 --> 00:08:43,850
positive, neutral, or negative.

170
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:47,095
So here you also had
features based on the words.

171
00:08:47,095 --> 00:08:48,720
And of course, your
classification task

172
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:54,760
was pretty simple, just
say 0, 1, 2 classification.

173
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:57,740
Later, similarly to the
direction I described,

174
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:00,780
we moved to going a
bit more to the source.

175
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:03,680
So language is, of course, very
sequential and context rich.

176
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:06,880
If I give you a random sentence
like the quick brown fox

177
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,713
jumps over the,
you'll know what comes

178
00:09:08,713 --> 00:09:10,880
next because you've seen
that sentence so many times

179
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:11,760
in your life.

180
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:15,480
So similarly, we started
training language models

181
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:17,340
to do next token prediction.

182
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:22,280
This also allowed you to make
maximum use of raw text data

183
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:25,440
because you could generate
a very large amount of pairs

184
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:28,120
like this.

185
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:30,540
And you might have guessed,
vision was no different.

186
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:32,560
Directions were pretty
similar, so things

187
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:36,000
like autoencoders to try to
generate those representations

188
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,245
that could be applied
downstream, and also

189
00:09:38,245 --> 00:09:39,620
things like
contrastive learning,

190
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:40,828
which we'll talk about later.

191
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,840
With vision, one
recentish architecture

192
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:49,260
is the masked autoencoder.

193
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:51,500
So the same idea I
described, except in vision,

194
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,800
where you mask out
some part of the image

195
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:56,000
and train the model to
reconstruct those missing

196
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,520
pieces.

197
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:01,120
One interesting question here
is how do you do the masking.

198
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:02,940
Typically, it's done randomly.

199
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:05,400
However, you could
have a random mask

200
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:07,400
that reveals three
patches like this, which

201
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:08,900
makes the problem very easy.

202
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:12,800
Even a human could tell or
these other three patches, which

203
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:14,800
makes the problem very hard.

204
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:18,400
So one future
direction is to find

205
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:21,840
a better balance between these.

206
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:26,280
Going back to language, a key
component of language models

207
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:27,600
is word embeddings.

208
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:29,780
So we understand language
pretty naturally,

209
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:31,440
but models work in numbers.

210
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,720
So in order to bridge this
gap, you need dense vectors

211
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:36,680
in a high dimensional
space that allow

212
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:41,360
you to represent all the
words in the vocabulary.

213
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:44,400
Typical methods in this space
are like Word2Vec, GloVe,

214
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:47,040
and FastTex, which you
can look at in case

215
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,360
you haven't come across them.

216
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:52,800
And these enable things like
arithmetic and, of course,

217
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:54,520
learning in this space.

218
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:56,400
For instance,
semantic similarity,

219
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:59,740
if you take king
minus man plus woman,

220
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:02,360
you're embedding should
roughly give you queen, which

221
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:04,160
is the analogous word there.

222
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:05,760
However, static
embeddings in which

223
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:09,840
you assign a single vector to
one word have some limitations.

224
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:13,200
For instance, bank as
a financial institution

225
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:15,420
and a river bank would
get the same vector,

226
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:18,520
even though their meanings
are completely different.

227
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:20,400
So to solve this
problem, there are

228
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:22,200
things like
contextual embeddings

229
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,200
which consider the
surrounding context

230
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,420
to assign different
vectors to the same word.

231
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,020
Moving a bit closer
to transformers,

232
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:34,060
we got to RNNs, Recurrent
Neural Networks.

233
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,520
And these are sequence
models that process inputs

234
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:40,950
step by step while maintaining
a memory or hidden state.

235
00:11:40,950 --> 00:11:45,730
So you take your input, you
do some operations on it,

236
00:11:45,730 --> 00:11:48,830
you put some information in
memory and some information

237
00:11:48,830 --> 00:11:49,650
to the output.

238
00:11:49,650 --> 00:11:53,732
You can repeat this over
time to get a sequence.

239
00:11:53,732 --> 00:11:55,440
However, these suffer
from some problems,

240
00:11:55,440 --> 00:11:57,930
like if your sequence
is very long,

241
00:11:57,930 --> 00:12:01,230
you're going to forget what you
learned at the very first step.

242
00:12:01,230 --> 00:12:05,470
So Long, Short Term
Memory Networks or LSTMs

243
00:12:05,470 --> 00:12:07,590
are a gated variant
of RNNs that aim

244
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:10,110
to solve this problem
by better preserving

245
00:12:10,110 --> 00:12:11,810
long-range dependencies.

246
00:12:11,810 --> 00:12:14,350
So here you add a
couple more parameters

247
00:12:14,350 --> 00:12:19,470
that better govern when you
should remember or forget

248
00:12:19,470 --> 00:12:23,990
something and when you
should output something.

249
00:12:23,990 --> 00:12:26,110
So then we get to transformers.

250
00:12:26,110 --> 00:12:27,570
I'll give a very quick overview.

251
00:12:27,570 --> 00:12:31,950
So self-attention learns what
to focus on for each token.

252
00:12:31,950 --> 00:12:34,510
So if we have a
sequence, the connections

253
00:12:34,510 --> 00:12:38,490
between different words might
be more or less important.

254
00:12:38,490 --> 00:12:43,190
So self-attention learns some
matrices, query, key, and value

255
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:46,830
that allow you to learn
these relationships.

256
00:12:46,830 --> 00:12:51,130
To give an analogy for these,
make it a little less abstract,

257
00:12:51,130 --> 00:12:53,910
imagine you're looking
for a book in a library.

258
00:12:53,910 --> 00:12:58,590
Each one has a summary, a key
that tells you what it's about.

259
00:12:58,590 --> 00:13:01,220
Once you find a match between
what you're looking for

260
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:03,390
or your query, and
the summary, you

261
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:07,350
can access the book to, well,
get its information, the values.

262
00:13:07,350 --> 00:13:10,310
And in attention, we do this
across, say, all the books

263
00:13:10,310 --> 00:13:12,790
in the library and
do a soft matching

264
00:13:12,790 --> 00:13:15,510
to get the book that
is most relevant.

265
00:13:15,510 --> 00:13:18,230
So you'd get a score for
every book telling you

266
00:13:18,230 --> 00:13:21,910
how relevant it is.

267
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:24,150
And self-attention is
just this technique

268
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:27,610
applied to a single sequence.

269
00:13:27,610 --> 00:13:30,130
So you learn connections between
every word in a sentence,

270
00:13:30,130 --> 00:13:34,550
say, to every other
word in that sentence.

271
00:13:34,550 --> 00:13:38,420
One issue here is
that models that

272
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:41,340
just do this have no
notion of ordering.

273
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:43,860
So to get around this, we
add positional encodings

274
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:47,220
or positional embeddings which
just tell the model where

275
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:48,640
it's looking for each word.

276
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:49,920
So it's a very simple example.

277
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:51,600
You could just have a
one for the first word,

278
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:53,140
a two for the
second word, and on,

279
00:13:53,140 --> 00:13:56,140
and your model would then
overtraining, learn to associate

280
00:13:56,140 --> 00:14:00,380
these with the ordering
of the sequence.

281
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:05,318
And lastly, you can scale this
up, do multi-head attention.

282
00:14:05,318 --> 00:14:06,860
So on the same
sequence, you can have

283
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:10,580
multiple different
learned attention matrices

284
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:16,620
or models that together give you
a more detailed representation.

285
00:14:16,620 --> 00:14:18,280
So this is the
full architecture.

286
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:20,040
I won't get into
too much detail,

287
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:22,540
but essentially combines
all of the components

288
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:29,300
that I talked about into a very
well-performing architecture.

289
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:32,060
So we just talked about RNNs.

290
00:14:32,060 --> 00:14:36,900
Why are those not used
in the latest models?

291
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:40,260
While transformers are
better for various reasons,

292
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:43,040
a few here are that they
allow for parallelism.

293
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:46,860
So RNNs by design are
one step by one step.

294
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:50,460
So you have to process
sequences in order,

295
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:52,000
which makes things much slower.

296
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,020
Whereas in attention, you can
process your entire sequence

297
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:59,100
in one step and speed
this up on GPUs.

298
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:03,380
Additionally, although LSTMs
solve the vanishing gradients

299
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:05,980
and forgetting
problem a little bit,

300
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:09,120
transformers still handle
long contexts much better.

301
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:10,720
You can scale them
up much better.

302
00:15:10,720 --> 00:15:12,340
So these days, we
have models that

303
00:15:12,340 --> 00:15:17,220
can take in a million
tokens or to that order.

304
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:20,160
And lastly here,
like I allows you,

305
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:24,740
like I showed, to access all
previous tokens in the context

306
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:26,900
and draw connections
in between them

307
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:30,100
whereas RNNs and
LSTMs are limited

308
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:33,200
to whatever they can store in
memory in their hidden state.

309
00:15:36,140 --> 00:15:40,960
So with all of these
advancements in architectures,

310
00:15:40,960 --> 00:15:45,340
transformers today have
spread to all areas

311
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:47,700
of science and technology.

312
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:50,180
So we have LLMs, which all
of us probably use every day

313
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:52,580
like GPT-5, Claude, et cetera.

314
00:15:52,580 --> 00:15:56,420
We have many vision models that
are getting better and better,

315
00:15:56,420 --> 00:15:58,140
some even coming to your phone.

316
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:04,060
We have speech, biology, video,
robotics, and so much more.

317
00:16:04,060 --> 00:16:06,020
Large language
models in particular

318
00:16:06,020 --> 00:16:08,340
are just scaled up transformers.

319
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:09,980
So the architecture
I showed scaled

320
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:13,380
to billions of parameters,
incurring heavy computational

321
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:15,500
costs, like time and GPUs.

322
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:17,980
However, they perform very well.

323
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:20,620
Generally, they're
pre-trained on massive amounts

324
00:16:20,620 --> 00:16:22,060
of general text data.

325
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:25,580
So the internet, to put
it in a simple word,

326
00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:27,758
with the training
objective that I mentioned

327
00:16:27,758 --> 00:16:30,300
of next token prediction, so
given a sequence just predicting

328
00:16:30,300 --> 00:16:33,970
the next word, as you
scale transformers

329
00:16:33,970 --> 00:16:37,710
up as language models, a lot
of emergent abilities come up.

330
00:16:37,710 --> 00:16:40,210
So there's a paper
on this seminal paper

331
00:16:40,210 --> 00:16:42,250
on emergent abilities.

332
00:16:42,250 --> 00:16:45,710
But just by training
on general text data,

333
00:16:45,710 --> 00:16:48,010
you start to be able
to do math or reasoning

334
00:16:48,010 --> 00:16:49,690
or that sort of thing.

335
00:16:49,690 --> 00:16:57,050
And then like I just mentioned,
these large general models

336
00:16:57,050 --> 00:17:00,063
are also very good for a few
shot or zero shot learning.

337
00:17:00,063 --> 00:17:01,730
So you can give it a
completely new task

338
00:17:01,730 --> 00:17:03,490
that it has never seen before.

339
00:17:03,490 --> 00:17:05,630
But just because it's
seen so much data,

340
00:17:05,630 --> 00:17:08,089
it's able to generalize
and very quickly learn

341
00:17:08,089 --> 00:17:09,150
what you want it to do.

342
00:17:13,069 --> 00:17:15,130
So now I'll be talking
about pre-training

343
00:17:15,130 --> 00:17:16,970
and the importance of data.

344
00:17:16,970 --> 00:17:20,270
So pre-training is basically
the first phase of training.

345
00:17:20,270 --> 00:17:22,369
So you have a randomly
initialized model,

346
00:17:22,369 --> 00:17:26,849
and your goal is to get as much
knowledge and basic capabilities

347
00:17:26,849 --> 00:17:28,410
into it as you can.

348
00:17:28,410 --> 00:17:30,290
So typically, this
involves training

349
00:17:30,290 --> 00:17:34,930
on a large variety of
diverse internet text.

350
00:17:34,930 --> 00:17:36,810
Because so much
diverse text means

351
00:17:36,810 --> 00:17:40,970
the model is able to hopefully
learn a statistical distribution

352
00:17:40,970 --> 00:17:43,890
of language that is similar
to the natural distribution

353
00:17:43,890 --> 00:17:46,770
of human language.

354
00:17:46,770 --> 00:17:49,230
And, yeah, the
fundamental aspect,

355
00:17:49,230 --> 00:17:51,370
especially of pre-training,
is in the data.

356
00:17:51,370 --> 00:17:55,890
It's the fuel behind our
machine-learning and AI models,

357
00:17:55,890 --> 00:17:59,310
especially for LLMs that
are trained on so much text.

358
00:17:59,310 --> 00:18:04,610
So how do we maximally leverage
data given its importance?

359
00:18:04,610 --> 00:18:07,230
So we'll be talking about
a few projects here,

360
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:09,970
ranging from the smaller
scale to the larger scale.

361
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:14,850
So I'll be talking about
these two small or child scale

362
00:18:14,850 --> 00:18:18,890
language learning projects.

363
00:18:18,890 --> 00:18:22,010
And Karan we'll be talking
more about some larger scale

364
00:18:22,010 --> 00:18:23,970
projects involving
things like RAG

365
00:18:23,970 --> 00:18:28,760
considered pre-training
and curriculum learning.

366
00:18:28,760 --> 00:18:31,240
So I'll begin by talking
about small language

367
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:33,680
models and BabyLM.

368
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:37,480
So humans and neural
networks, language models,

369
00:18:37,480 --> 00:18:39,640
learn fundamentally differently.

370
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:41,800
Like I said, language
models are typically

371
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:44,440
trained on large amounts
of internet text.

372
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:48,200
They learn from predicting the
next token with the highest

373
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:52,480
probability, given all the
previous ones, whereas humans

374
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:55,400
likely learn in a more
structured, hierarchical ways.

375
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:59,240
For example, we are more
goal-based, goal-driven,

376
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:03,960
and our language is
decomposed, given

377
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:09,480
goals or steps that we want to
take to accomplish something.

378
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:13,740
Furthermore, we learn in
a more interactive manner.

379
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:15,360
So we talk to other people.

380
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:18,640
So as children growing up, we
talk to other kids, our mom,

381
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:20,480
babysitter, and so forth.

382
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:22,160
Whereas, again,
language models are

383
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:25,440
trained on just pure
text on the internet.

384
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:27,980
Furthermore, we're
multimodal and sensory.

385
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:31,860
We're continuously absorbing
information from the world,

386
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,920
and this grounds our
learning of language.

387
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:39,640
Whereas most language
models are simply text only.

388
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:42,160
And our focus of the two
projects I'll be talking about

389
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:44,840
are mainly the language
data differences

390
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:49,320
between human learning and
language model learning.

391
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:51,500
So why study small
models and data?

392
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,640
Why not just use the biggest
models that can do everything?

393
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:57,960
Well, firstly,
computational costs.

394
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:02,440
Not everybody can train a model
like OpenAI, GPT-4, GPT-5.

395
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:04,880
Takes millions of dollars.

396
00:20:04,880 --> 00:20:07,880
Furthermore, small models can
open up more potential use

397
00:20:07,880 --> 00:20:08,880
cases.

398
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:12,080
You can use it on your phone to
do things on your everyday life.

399
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:16,040
That might be much more of
aligned to your goals or tasks

400
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:20,680
compared to an open source, so
a closed source large language

401
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:23,080
model like GPT-5.

402
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:25,630
And one other thing is
techniques discovered

403
00:20:25,630 --> 00:20:27,430
at the small scale
can potentially

404
00:20:27,430 --> 00:20:30,150
be applied to larger scales
to improve their efficiency

405
00:20:30,150 --> 00:20:31,790
and capabilities as well.

406
00:20:31,790 --> 00:20:33,750
And lastly, a
greater understanding

407
00:20:33,750 --> 00:20:38,190
of how we can make small
language models more effective

408
00:20:38,190 --> 00:20:39,990
might also lead to
greater understanding

409
00:20:39,990 --> 00:20:43,110
of the cognitive models of how
humans actually learn language

410
00:20:43,110 --> 00:20:44,280
so efficiently.

411
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:46,030
So the first project
I'll be talking about

412
00:20:46,030 --> 00:20:49,470
is a recent paper we just
worked on called baby scale:

413
00:20:49,470 --> 00:20:52,750
investigating models trained on
individual children's language

414
00:20:52,750 --> 00:20:55,190
input.

415
00:20:55,190 --> 00:20:58,470
So human children, again,
learn from far less data

416
00:20:58,470 --> 00:21:01,330
than large language
models, magnitudes less.

417
00:21:01,330 --> 00:21:04,470
A human child from
the ages of 0 to 13

418
00:21:04,470 --> 00:21:09,110
is exposed to approximately 10
to 100 million words of language

419
00:21:09,110 --> 00:21:12,070
data, compared to large
language models in order

420
00:21:12,070 --> 00:21:14,890
to achieve similar capabilities
such as generalization,

421
00:21:14,890 --> 00:21:17,030
abstraction, and reasoning.

422
00:21:17,030 --> 00:21:21,390
So we wanted to ask, why
exactly is this the case?

423
00:21:21,390 --> 00:21:23,270
And thankfully,
we had a data set

424
00:21:23,270 --> 00:21:27,110
that actually had transcripts of
individual children's language

425
00:21:27,110 --> 00:21:27,610
data.

426
00:21:27,610 --> 00:21:30,150
So we were wondering, could
we train language models

427
00:21:30,150 --> 00:21:34,350
on the actual transcript data
that individual children are

428
00:21:34,350 --> 00:21:39,430
exposed to and what
they say as well,

429
00:21:39,430 --> 00:21:42,390
and see how that might
scale, as well as

430
00:21:42,390 --> 00:21:46,470
how performance might vary
across individual families,

431
00:21:46,470 --> 00:21:48,590
and also to look
at what properties

432
00:21:48,590 --> 00:21:52,190
of the actual data, language
data that children are exposed

433
00:21:52,190 --> 00:21:54,470
to might make them
more effective

434
00:21:54,470 --> 00:21:56,610
learners compared to others?

435
00:21:59,270 --> 00:22:00,830
So what we saw--

436
00:22:00,830 --> 00:22:04,510
so on the left actually is
a scaling plot across four

437
00:22:04,510 --> 00:22:05,870
different benchmarks.

438
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:09,190
So Zorro tests grammaticality
and linguistic abilities.

439
00:22:09,190 --> 00:22:11,010
WS is Word Similarity.

440
00:22:11,010 --> 00:22:13,310
So WS and comms test
semantic understanding.

441
00:22:13,310 --> 00:22:17,190
And EWoK tests general
basic world knowledge.

442
00:22:17,190 --> 00:22:19,310
So you see the four
graphs on the left

443
00:22:19,310 --> 00:22:23,470
are trained on our
individual family models.

444
00:22:23,470 --> 00:22:25,610
So you see there's quite
a lot of variation,

445
00:22:25,610 --> 00:22:27,890
but there is some
positive scaling.

446
00:22:27,890 --> 00:22:32,550
So even at such extremely
small or tiny scales of data,

447
00:22:32,550 --> 00:22:34,610
there is scaling with
the quantity of data.

448
00:22:34,610 --> 00:22:37,630
There is positive trends, but
the signal is quite noisy.

449
00:22:37,630 --> 00:22:41,350
And you'll see the performance
varies quite heavily

450
00:22:41,350 --> 00:22:44,310
between the individual models
trained on different families

451
00:22:44,310 --> 00:22:44,870
data.

452
00:22:44,870 --> 00:22:48,570
So on the right, we trained on
synthetic, tiny dialogues data.

453
00:22:48,570 --> 00:22:51,870
So this is a larger data
up to 200 million tokens.

454
00:22:51,870 --> 00:22:53,770
And it's synthetic and cleaner.

455
00:22:53,770 --> 00:22:57,230
So you'll see with this cleaner
more synthetic controlled

456
00:22:57,230 --> 00:23:04,270
data, the scaling laws are
cleaner and hold better there.

457
00:23:04,270 --> 00:23:09,390
So if we were to extrapolate and
look at broader scaling curves,

458
00:23:09,390 --> 00:23:12,910
so up to Roberta base, which
was pre-trained on, I believe,

459
00:23:12,910 --> 00:23:15,890
around $20 or $30
billion tokens,

460
00:23:15,890 --> 00:23:22,140
you'll see that our smaller
experiments do lie along

461
00:23:22,140 --> 00:23:24,120
the broader scaling curves.

462
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:30,060
So again, I said, the scaling--

463
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:31,700
one other thing I
forgot to mention

464
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:33,660
is the scaling does
depend on the task.

465
00:23:33,660 --> 00:23:36,180
So it scaled better for
Zorro or grammaticality

466
00:23:36,180 --> 00:23:38,460
compared to a moral
knowledge, for example.

467
00:23:38,460 --> 00:23:40,500
So it shows that individual
children's learning

468
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:43,080
environments, at least when
training language models,

469
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:47,380
might not be enough to get
general basic world model

470
00:23:47,380 --> 00:23:49,340
a world understanding,
and you might

471
00:23:49,340 --> 00:23:53,540
need more extraneous or external
data in order to achieve that.

472
00:23:53,540 --> 00:23:57,080
And the other
takeaways I discussed.

473
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:02,060
Furthermore, we conducted
a very comprehensive

474
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:05,500
linguistic analysis of these
individual families training

475
00:24:05,500 --> 00:24:07,620
data, comparing--

476
00:24:07,620 --> 00:24:10,620
correlating them to the final
performance of the models

477
00:24:10,620 --> 00:24:11,700
trained on their data.

478
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:16,200
And we found that it wasn't
just data scale that mattered,

479
00:24:16,200 --> 00:24:18,440
but a lot of other
qualities, for example,

480
00:24:18,440 --> 00:24:23,300
things related to semantic
diversity and the number

481
00:24:23,300 --> 00:24:26,820
of conversations, the
distributional divergence

482
00:24:26,820 --> 00:24:30,480
among the different individual
data sets of each family,

483
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:33,020
and so forth.

484
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:35,300
So overall, we found that
better performing data sets

485
00:24:35,300 --> 00:24:38,740
tended to be more structured and
more diverse and richer in terms

486
00:24:38,740 --> 00:24:40,980
of interaction and
coverage diversity.

487
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:42,620
So this lines up
with child language

488
00:24:42,620 --> 00:24:45,300
of research, emphasizing
that quality really

489
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:49,900
matters more than just quantity,
especially at such small scales.

490
00:24:49,900 --> 00:24:51,940
So overall, child
scale language model

491
00:24:51,940 --> 00:24:56,100
learning on such extremely
limited amounts of data

492
00:24:56,100 --> 00:24:57,540
is possible.

493
00:24:57,540 --> 00:24:59,580
But it's quite
task-dependent, and it really

494
00:24:59,580 --> 00:25:03,140
depends on the quality as
well as the other aspects

495
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:04,700
of your data, more
than the amount

496
00:25:04,700 --> 00:25:07,820
of data at such small scales.

497
00:25:07,820 --> 00:25:10,300
And studying child
scale data in general

498
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:13,060
might allow us to build
more capable and efficient

499
00:25:13,060 --> 00:25:15,140
small language
models and possibly

500
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:17,770
also allow us to
understand human language

501
00:25:17,770 --> 00:25:20,330
acquisition better.

502
00:25:20,330 --> 00:25:23,010
So the second project
I'll be talking about

503
00:25:23,010 --> 00:25:25,090
is also at smaller
scales, but it's

504
00:25:25,090 --> 00:25:29,090
investigating training bilingual
or multilingual small language

505
00:25:29,090 --> 00:25:30,770
models.

506
00:25:30,770 --> 00:25:33,370
So in particular, this is
a thing called a fusion

507
00:25:33,370 --> 00:25:36,370
hypothesis, mainly
applying to humans, which

508
00:25:36,370 --> 00:25:39,770
is a hypothesis that human
children that grow up

509
00:25:39,770 --> 00:25:42,570
in multilingual
environments might actually

510
00:25:42,570 --> 00:25:44,950
suffer more or have more
difficulties learning,

511
00:25:44,950 --> 00:25:48,570
since they're constantly
required to balance or take

512
00:25:48,570 --> 00:25:50,530
in so much different
conflict information

513
00:25:50,530 --> 00:25:53,770
from different languages,
and this might actually

514
00:25:53,770 --> 00:25:56,050
decrease their
capabilities of learning,

515
00:25:56,050 --> 00:25:57,610
a single primary language.

516
00:25:57,610 --> 00:26:00,770
So we're wondering, do
these things actually

517
00:26:00,770 --> 00:26:03,850
apply, especially
for language models?

518
00:26:03,850 --> 00:26:10,290
And secondly, we're wondering
if the exact exposure structure

519
00:26:10,290 --> 00:26:16,130
or the way that this bilingual
language is expressed

520
00:26:16,130 --> 00:26:19,090
to the model or the child,
does this affect performance?

521
00:26:19,090 --> 00:26:21,130
And thirdly, do these
effects vary with things

522
00:26:21,130 --> 00:26:24,450
like the model and data size?

523
00:26:24,450 --> 00:26:26,050
So in order to do
so, we constructed

524
00:26:26,050 --> 00:26:28,890
these different
types of data sets.

525
00:26:28,890 --> 00:26:31,930
So we have some top lines, which
are hundreds million tokens

526
00:26:31,930 --> 00:26:33,770
of English and Spanish data.

527
00:26:33,770 --> 00:26:36,090
And then we have
some baseline models,

528
00:26:36,090 --> 00:26:38,890
which are half of this
in terms of English only.

529
00:26:38,890 --> 00:26:41,890
And then on top of this,
we added multilingual data

530
00:26:41,890 --> 00:26:45,430
in a second language,
Spanish in this case.

531
00:26:45,430 --> 00:26:48,570
So it's 100 million tokens
mixed 50 million of English

532
00:26:48,570 --> 00:26:50,210
and 50 million of Spanish.

533
00:26:50,210 --> 00:26:51,930
And this is
simultaneously learned.

534
00:26:51,930 --> 00:26:55,010
We're not training on 50 million
English and then 50 million

535
00:26:55,010 --> 00:26:59,610
Spanish, but rather, there's
100 million tokens together.

536
00:26:59,610 --> 00:27:01,110
And we also look
at code switching,

537
00:27:01,110 --> 00:27:03,930
which is the interleaving of the
two different languages at both

538
00:27:03,930 --> 00:27:05,930
a sentence, as well
as a word level

539
00:27:05,930 --> 00:27:09,730
and how that might
affect learning.

540
00:27:09,730 --> 00:27:12,750
So in terms of the
first research question,

541
00:27:12,750 --> 00:27:16,170
we found that the perplexities
of the multilingual model

542
00:27:16,170 --> 00:27:20,770
are low in both languages
and very comparable, even

543
00:27:20,770 --> 00:27:23,110
very slightly lower than
the English baseline,

544
00:27:23,110 --> 00:27:24,970
showing that adding
a second language

545
00:27:24,970 --> 00:27:27,770
doesn't really affect
too much behavior

546
00:27:27,770 --> 00:27:30,130
or learning of the
first language.

547
00:27:30,130 --> 00:27:32,110
And this is also shown
through our benchmarks

548
00:27:32,110 --> 00:27:34,690
the multilingual models
perform pretty on par

549
00:27:34,690 --> 00:27:37,990
across different seeds compared
to the English-only models.

550
00:27:41,770 --> 00:27:45,330
And secondly, again, we looked
at different exposure schemes.

551
00:27:45,330 --> 00:27:49,290
For example, should
having the mom always talk

552
00:27:49,290 --> 00:27:51,150
in English and dad
always talk in Spanish

553
00:27:51,150 --> 00:27:55,170
versus having them be random by
Speaker and so forth, as well

554
00:27:55,170 --> 00:27:57,950
as sentence versus word
level code switching?

555
00:27:57,950 --> 00:28:00,870
And again, we found no
significant differences here.

556
00:28:03,570 --> 00:28:05,810
And thirdly, we looked
at how these effects

557
00:28:05,810 --> 00:28:07,930
may vary with scale.

558
00:28:07,930 --> 00:28:10,090
So we found that
using a smaller model

559
00:28:10,090 --> 00:28:11,780
didn't really
affect performance,

560
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:13,760
whereas dropping
down all the way

561
00:28:13,760 --> 00:28:16,560
down to 20 million words
instead of 100 million words

562
00:28:16,560 --> 00:28:18,380
did significantly
affect performance.

563
00:28:18,380 --> 00:28:21,240
But overall, our
trends held up there.

564
00:28:21,240 --> 00:28:24,440
So overall, we find
there's no interference.

565
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:28,580
So training small language
models on, for example,

566
00:28:28,580 --> 00:28:31,240
two languages doesn't really
impact or negatively affect

567
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:36,640
the performance on a single
language, which is quite cool.

568
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:40,020
Further, the exposure structure
is surprisingly irrelevant.

569
00:28:40,020 --> 00:28:42,560
It doesn't really matter how
exactly you interleave the two

570
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:43,840
languages.

571
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:47,160
It doesn't really degrade
learning of the models.

572
00:28:47,160 --> 00:28:50,160
And data scale matters
more than model scale

573
00:28:50,160 --> 00:28:52,742
for multilingual
learning at this size.

574
00:28:52,742 --> 00:28:54,200
So those are the
overall takeaways.

575
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:56,100
And feel free to
check out the paper.

576
00:28:59,745 --> 00:29:01,120
Next I'll be
talking a little bit

577
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:04,160
about work in retrieval
augmented generation.

578
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:08,080
So firstly, what is Retrieval
Augmented Generation or RAG?

579
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:10,780
So typically, like we
described, in language models,

580
00:29:10,780 --> 00:29:12,900
you just have this
large set of web data.

581
00:29:12,900 --> 00:29:18,240
You train your LLM to model that
data and go from user queries

582
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:19,320
to outputs.

583
00:29:19,320 --> 00:29:22,520
In RAG, we augment the
system a little bit

584
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:25,320
by adding some specific
or domain knowledge

585
00:29:25,320 --> 00:29:27,360
documents with a retriever.

586
00:29:27,360 --> 00:29:30,600
Whenever you get a user
query, your retriever

587
00:29:30,600 --> 00:29:33,760
picks out of this large set of
documents the most relevant ones

588
00:29:33,760 --> 00:29:36,500
and feeds them to
the LLMs context,

589
00:29:36,500 --> 00:29:42,160
which it can then use to improve
the outputs that it generates.

590
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:45,360
So one of our works was
studying RAG scaling.

591
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:46,960
So how much RAG should you use?

592
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:50,200
If you have a fixed set of,
say, hundreds billion tokens,

593
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:54,400
what allocations should you
do for both of those things?

594
00:29:54,400 --> 00:29:56,240
And if you plotted
retrieval tokens

595
00:29:56,240 --> 00:29:58,240
in the middle versus
pre-training tokens,

596
00:29:58,240 --> 00:30:02,400
for every budget, there would
be some optimal, which would

597
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:04,920
form this optimal frontier.

598
00:30:04,920 --> 00:30:07,420
A very quick overview of
the results, on the right,

599
00:30:07,420 --> 00:30:10,510
we found that small models
benefit much more from RAG.

600
00:30:10,510 --> 00:30:13,350
So as you scale up
RAG, their performance

601
00:30:13,350 --> 00:30:16,890
improves, whereas larger
models saturate very quickly.

602
00:30:19,510 --> 00:30:24,190
So described in figure form,
if we plot pre-training tokens

603
00:30:24,190 --> 00:30:27,350
saved per retrieval token--
so essentially, we're

604
00:30:27,350 --> 00:30:31,550
looking at how much
pre-training does it

605
00:30:31,550 --> 00:30:37,470
take to match the performance
gain from retrieval or vice

606
00:30:37,470 --> 00:30:38,270
versa?

607
00:30:38,270 --> 00:30:40,230
And when looking at
this, we found that there

608
00:30:40,230 --> 00:30:41,598
was this crossover point.

609
00:30:41,598 --> 00:30:43,390
So there's a minimum
amount of pre-training

610
00:30:43,390 --> 00:30:46,230
that you need to do in order for
your model to effectively use

611
00:30:46,230 --> 00:30:47,830
any RAG that you give it.

612
00:30:47,830 --> 00:30:51,190
And this occurs for us at
about four pre-training tokens

613
00:30:51,190 --> 00:30:52,630
per parameter.

614
00:30:52,630 --> 00:30:54,570
So if you have a 1
billion parameter model,

615
00:30:54,570 --> 00:30:57,470
you need about 4 billion
tokens to reach that point.

616
00:30:57,470 --> 00:31:00,750
Also on the right, we
found that the improvement

617
00:31:00,750 --> 00:31:02,750
per 1 billion
retrieval tokens varied

618
00:31:02,750 --> 00:31:04,270
by the scale of the model.

619
00:31:04,270 --> 00:31:08,310
So a very small model,
like 30 million parameters,

620
00:31:08,310 --> 00:31:10,310
benefits much more
from RAG compared

621
00:31:10,310 --> 00:31:13,550
to a large model like 3
billion parameters, which

622
00:31:13,550 --> 00:31:16,250
would sufficient pre-training
almost has zero benefit.

623
00:31:16,250 --> 00:31:19,470
It's going down on the
graph, as you can see.

624
00:31:19,470 --> 00:31:23,027
This also makes sense because
a large model is already

625
00:31:23,027 --> 00:31:25,110
memorized so much information
that it can't really

626
00:31:25,110 --> 00:31:28,290
use RAG with general web data.

627
00:31:28,290 --> 00:31:31,950
However, of course, if you
had domain specific documents,

628
00:31:31,950 --> 00:31:34,870
then that would be
a different story.

629
00:31:34,870 --> 00:31:36,370
Very quick overview
of this project,

630
00:31:36,370 --> 00:31:40,530
but if this seems interesting
to you, you can scan this QR.

631
00:31:40,530 --> 00:31:41,030
Oops.

632
00:31:50,030 --> 00:31:52,510
Next I'll talk a
bit about curriculum

633
00:31:52,510 --> 00:31:56,790
learning approaches and
scaling up models using this.

634
00:31:56,790 --> 00:31:59,390
So like Steven
was talking about,

635
00:31:59,390 --> 00:32:00,970
as the cost of
pre-training grows,

636
00:32:00,970 --> 00:32:04,230
there is more interest in
improving learning efficiency

637
00:32:04,230 --> 00:32:07,030
and trying to train models with
less than a trillion tokens

638
00:32:07,030 --> 00:32:09,750
or the insane scales
that are used these days.

639
00:32:09,750 --> 00:32:12,470
And also typical pre-training
differs significantly

640
00:32:12,470 --> 00:32:16,150
from how we learn, and also
can result in small models

641
00:32:16,150 --> 00:32:17,630
that struggle at reasoning.

642
00:32:17,630 --> 00:32:19,970
So how can we
bridge these scales.

643
00:32:19,970 --> 00:32:26,990
One idea I had was we as humans
learn from curriculum in school.

644
00:32:26,990 --> 00:32:28,550
Alongside that,
however, our brains

645
00:32:28,550 --> 00:32:30,410
are also growing
physically in size.

646
00:32:30,410 --> 00:32:32,410
So can we do the same
thing for language models,

647
00:32:32,410 --> 00:32:35,470
start with a small model
and very simple data

648
00:32:35,470 --> 00:32:37,250
and scale up both
things in tandem,

649
00:32:37,250 --> 00:32:41,070
so growing the model as
the data gets harder.

650
00:32:41,070 --> 00:32:44,550
Some prior work in this space
has been like synthetic data

651
00:32:44,550 --> 00:32:46,530
sets like Steven
used tiny dialogues,

652
00:32:46,530 --> 00:32:48,250
there's also tiny stories.

653
00:32:48,250 --> 00:32:52,750
So very simple, like
five-year-old level stories.

654
00:32:52,750 --> 00:32:55,090
There's some work in
data filtering as well.

655
00:32:55,090 --> 00:32:58,430
So on that quality end,
curating large data

656
00:32:58,430 --> 00:33:04,660
sets that are deduplicated
and very clean.

657
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:08,180
On the model growing end, there
is some prior work in stacking.

658
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:12,940
So if we take a small model,
train it on whatever amount

659
00:33:12,940 --> 00:33:17,500
of data and then copy
the encoder layers

660
00:33:17,500 --> 00:33:22,420
and train further, does
that improve performance?

661
00:33:22,420 --> 00:33:24,620
And this other paper,
Midas, from 2024

662
00:33:24,620 --> 00:33:27,180
showed that when you
do this stacking,

663
00:33:27,180 --> 00:33:28,620
layers that are
next to each other

664
00:33:28,620 --> 00:33:30,360
essentially learn
the same things.

665
00:33:30,360 --> 00:33:32,540
So they're not
very useful, which

666
00:33:32,540 --> 00:33:34,900
motivates new or better
approaches to doing

667
00:33:34,900 --> 00:33:38,420
this stacking or model growth.

668
00:33:38,420 --> 00:33:40,960
So what we came up with
was, like I mentioned,

669
00:33:40,960 --> 00:33:42,820
starting off with
small model, say,

670
00:33:42,820 --> 00:33:45,600
half the model size and
relatively easy data,

671
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:48,060
training it a little
bit, and gradually

672
00:33:48,060 --> 00:33:51,340
adding layers as you go
throughout training until you

673
00:33:51,340 --> 00:33:53,300
reach your final
model size, which you

674
00:33:53,300 --> 00:33:56,900
can train on your hardest
data to do the tasks

675
00:33:56,900 --> 00:33:59,980
that you want to.

676
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:02,740
And we found that doing
this approach, which

677
00:34:02,740 --> 00:34:05,940
we call CGLS, Curriculum
Guided Layer Scaling,

678
00:34:05,940 --> 00:34:07,980
you do achieve better
performance than just

679
00:34:07,980 --> 00:34:11,860
training randomly your largest
model size on all of your data

680
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:14,280
or even using the
same curriculum,

681
00:34:14,280 --> 00:34:17,580
so a easy to hard scaling,
but at the largest model

682
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:19,460
size train ones.

683
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:21,159
And these results also scale up.

684
00:34:21,159 --> 00:34:25,420
So if we move from 2 billion
to 20 billion tokens,

685
00:34:25,420 --> 00:34:28,120
the gap in performance
gets even larger,

686
00:34:28,120 --> 00:34:29,239
so the model gets better.

687
00:34:32,580 --> 00:34:38,139
So as a TLDR, testing at these
124 million parameter and 1B

688
00:34:38,139 --> 00:34:43,659
scales, combining model growth
with a structured curriculum

689
00:34:43,659 --> 00:34:47,340
allows you to unlock the
benefits of model growth

690
00:34:47,340 --> 00:34:48,820
or stacking techniques.

691
00:34:48,820 --> 00:34:52,179
But not all tasks benefited
equally from this approach

692
00:34:52,179 --> 00:34:54,219
and curriculum.

693
00:34:54,219 --> 00:34:57,540
And although I didn't show it
here at 1 billion parameter

694
00:34:57,540 --> 00:35:00,290
scale, the perplexity
of the random baseline

695
00:35:00,290 --> 00:35:04,030
was better than
that of the CGLS 1,

696
00:35:04,030 --> 00:35:09,570
despite the CGLS 1 being
better at reasoning tasks.

697
00:35:09,570 --> 00:35:12,570
And there's, of course, a lot of
future potential in this space,

698
00:35:12,570 --> 00:35:15,330
like tuning
hyperparameters further,

699
00:35:15,330 --> 00:35:17,630
exploring other
notions of curriculum.

700
00:35:17,630 --> 00:35:20,290
So maybe what we
think is easy or hard

701
00:35:20,290 --> 00:35:22,730
might not be easy or
hard for language model

702
00:35:22,730 --> 00:35:25,210
and applying this to
other domains like image

703
00:35:25,210 --> 00:35:29,970
or medical imaging
and that thing.

704
00:35:29,970 --> 00:35:33,910
And like the other one's, paper,
if you'd like to check it out.

705
00:35:37,990 --> 00:35:38,490
Yeah.

706
00:35:38,490 --> 00:35:40,782
So now we'll talk about some
of these overall takeaways

707
00:35:40,782 --> 00:35:45,330
from these four projects,
studying data, especially

708
00:35:45,330 --> 00:35:47,130
for pre-training.

709
00:35:47,130 --> 00:35:49,930
So overall, we see that data
effectiveness is not just

710
00:35:49,930 --> 00:35:53,370
the volume and quantity of data,
but the quality, structure,

711
00:35:53,370 --> 00:35:56,010
and how exactly it's used.

712
00:35:56,010 --> 00:36:00,170
So data selection and how you
actually use it strategically

713
00:36:00,170 --> 00:36:01,930
are very important
considerations.

714
00:36:01,930 --> 00:36:05,130
So we saw with the
baby scale project

715
00:36:05,130 --> 00:36:08,130
that individual environments
of children's learning

716
00:36:08,130 --> 00:36:11,010
vary, and the differences are
driven more by data composition

717
00:36:11,010 --> 00:36:12,850
and quality than quantity.

718
00:36:12,850 --> 00:36:15,250
And with the
bilingual project, we

719
00:36:15,250 --> 00:36:18,970
saw that models can, too,
learn two languages effectively

720
00:36:18,970 --> 00:36:22,010
without confusion or detriment
to the first language,

721
00:36:22,010 --> 00:36:27,530
regardless of how it's exposed
to those two languages.

722
00:36:27,530 --> 00:36:31,330
Third, with the curriculum
guided on their scaling project

723
00:36:31,330 --> 00:36:33,090
that Karan just
talked about, we saw

724
00:36:33,090 --> 00:36:36,050
that scaling your model
alongside a data curriculum

725
00:36:36,050 --> 00:36:38,570
improves downstream
performance up to the 1 billion

726
00:36:38,570 --> 00:36:39,650
parameter scale.

727
00:36:39,650 --> 00:36:42,090
And with the right considerate
pre-training scaling laws

728
00:36:42,090 --> 00:36:45,050
project, we saw that
the optimal performance

729
00:36:45,050 --> 00:36:47,530
comes from balancing
parametric learning

730
00:36:47,530 --> 00:36:50,410
through pre-training
and external memory

731
00:36:50,410 --> 00:36:52,930
through retrieval.

732
00:36:52,930 --> 00:36:58,490
So now, so the overall takeaway
is that all of this research

733
00:36:58,490 --> 00:37:01,650
underscores that effective
language modeling isn't just

734
00:37:01,650 --> 00:37:04,730
amassing large amounts of data,
but smarter data utilization

735
00:37:04,730 --> 00:37:07,410
strategies that can
really effectively harness

736
00:37:07,410 --> 00:37:10,050
its structure, quality,
and characteristics.

737
00:37:10,050 --> 00:37:12,050
And by continuing to
refine and explore

738
00:37:12,050 --> 00:37:15,970
these data-centric approaches,
especially for pre-training,

739
00:37:15,970 --> 00:37:19,210
the future of LLM
training promises

740
00:37:19,210 --> 00:37:24,410
smarter, more efficient,
and adaptable models.

741
00:37:24,410 --> 00:37:27,610
Now, we're going to get to
the second major topic, which

742
00:37:27,610 --> 00:37:28,710
is post-training.

743
00:37:31,690 --> 00:37:35,010
So after we've
effectively pre-trained

744
00:37:35,010 --> 00:37:37,350
a randomly initialized
model, for example,

745
00:37:37,350 --> 00:37:41,210
on a lot of internet data, and
it has basic capabilities, what

746
00:37:41,210 --> 00:37:41,770
now?

747
00:37:41,770 --> 00:37:45,370
How do we adapt it to specific
task, scenarios, users, domains,

748
00:37:45,370 --> 00:37:46,430
and et cetera?

749
00:37:46,430 --> 00:37:49,550
So this is exactly what
post-training looks at.

750
00:37:49,550 --> 00:37:52,170
So there's many strategies,
like fine-tuning,

751
00:37:52,170 --> 00:37:55,560
prompt-based techniques,
and what Karan somewhat

752
00:37:55,560 --> 00:37:58,180
talked about, retrieval-based
approaches, like RAG.

753
00:38:02,040 --> 00:38:05,520
So there's a major of inference
time-based approach called

754
00:38:05,520 --> 00:38:07,320
chain of thought prompting.

755
00:38:07,320 --> 00:38:09,620
So this is when
you want the model,

756
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:12,040
for example, to talk to you
or to answer a question,

757
00:38:12,040 --> 00:38:14,300
instead of outputting
the answer directly,

758
00:38:14,300 --> 00:38:17,080
you get it to actually think
step by step like a human

759
00:38:17,080 --> 00:38:18,960
before answering a question.

760
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:22,400
So, for example, we decompose
more difficult problems

761
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:26,340
like math questions into
smaller subproblems or steps.

762
00:38:26,340 --> 00:38:29,560
And that's how we reason
through them and solve them.

763
00:38:29,560 --> 00:38:31,363
And this paper chain
of thought reasoning

764
00:38:31,363 --> 00:38:33,280
basically finds that you
can do the same thing

765
00:38:33,280 --> 00:38:35,200
with language models.

766
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:37,540
This suggests that deep
in the model's weights,

767
00:38:37,540 --> 00:38:40,240
it knows more about
the problem than it's

768
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:44,697
letting on when you just prompt
it to give you the answer.

769
00:38:44,697 --> 00:38:45,780
So here's just an example.

770
00:38:45,780 --> 00:38:47,680
You have a basic math problem.

771
00:38:47,680 --> 00:38:49,380
Standard prompting
directly gives you

772
00:38:49,380 --> 00:38:50,880
the answer, whereas
chain of thought

773
00:38:50,880 --> 00:38:53,960
has the model actually think
and output its reasoning

774
00:38:53,960 --> 00:38:58,000
steps before it generates
the final answer conditioned

775
00:38:58,000 --> 00:39:01,080
on these reasoning steps.

776
00:39:01,080 --> 00:39:02,470
Now, there's been
different works

777
00:39:02,470 --> 00:39:04,220
that extend chain of
thought, for example,

778
00:39:04,220 --> 00:39:05,300
to tree of thoughts.

779
00:39:05,300 --> 00:39:07,480
So rather than a
single reasoning path,

780
00:39:07,480 --> 00:39:09,760
the model considers
multiple reasoning paths

781
00:39:09,760 --> 00:39:14,100
and actually evaluates which one
is more effective, for example,

782
00:39:14,100 --> 00:39:18,560
using methods like
majority vote and so forth.

783
00:39:18,560 --> 00:39:22,200
Furthermore, you can offload
your intermediate reasoning

784
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:25,120
steps to other tools or
things like programs.

785
00:39:25,120 --> 00:39:27,040
For example, you
can generate code

786
00:39:27,040 --> 00:39:29,120
as intermediate reasoning
steps, which can then

787
00:39:29,120 --> 00:39:31,760
be used to help solve
them, for example, using

788
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:35,600
Python or some other coding
language or interpreter.

789
00:39:35,600 --> 00:39:40,960
And this helps provide a more
precise reasoning steps as well

790
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:43,460
as final answers leading
to higher performance,

791
00:39:43,460 --> 00:39:47,360
especially on things like,
for example, mathematics.

792
00:39:47,360 --> 00:39:50,555
Furthermore, like
I said, typically,

793
00:39:50,555 --> 00:39:51,930
when there's a
very hard problem,

794
00:39:51,930 --> 00:39:54,470
we would want to decompose
them into smaller,

795
00:39:54,470 --> 00:39:55,770
easier subproblems.

796
00:39:55,770 --> 00:40:00,870
And that's exactly what
socratic questioning does.

797
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:02,670
So it uses a
self-questioning module

798
00:40:02,670 --> 00:40:06,070
to use the language model to
propose subproblems related

799
00:40:06,070 --> 00:40:07,290
to the original problem.

800
00:40:07,290 --> 00:40:10,590
And then it uses a divide
and conquer algorithm

801
00:40:10,590 --> 00:40:14,510
to solve these subproblems
recursively, and then

802
00:40:14,510 --> 00:40:18,773
get an answer to your
overall original problem.

803
00:40:18,773 --> 00:40:20,190
There's also other
works that look

804
00:40:20,190 --> 00:40:22,790
at things like decomposing--

805
00:40:22,790 --> 00:40:25,890
formulating compositional
tasks as computation graphs.

806
00:40:25,890 --> 00:40:27,910
Again, this is similar
to breaking things up

807
00:40:27,910 --> 00:40:32,350
into subprocedures
or subproblems.

808
00:40:32,350 --> 00:40:36,230
And next, there's another line
of post-training approaches

809
00:40:36,230 --> 00:40:39,190
which use reinforcement
learning and rewards.

810
00:40:39,190 --> 00:40:41,830
So the most common
is reinforcement

811
00:40:41,830 --> 00:40:43,470
learning with human feedback.

812
00:40:43,470 --> 00:40:47,030
So this is a technique that
trains a reward model directly

813
00:40:47,030 --> 00:40:48,230
from human feedback.

814
00:40:48,230 --> 00:40:51,510
For example, if you have
two different responses

815
00:40:51,510 --> 00:40:53,630
that a model gives, you
give this to a human

816
00:40:53,630 --> 00:40:56,070
and get them to rank or
choose which response

817
00:40:56,070 --> 00:40:57,670
they prefer over
the other, and this

818
00:40:57,670 --> 00:41:00,670
can be used as a reward in
order to post train your model

819
00:41:00,670 --> 00:41:04,110
to be more effective.

820
00:41:04,110 --> 00:41:07,630
Furthermore, one
innovation is it's

821
00:41:07,630 --> 00:41:10,450
called DPO or Direct
Preference Optimization.

822
00:41:10,450 --> 00:41:12,850
So this is a RL-free
based approach.

823
00:41:12,850 --> 00:41:15,950
So it directly trains the model
to prefer human outputs in order

824
00:41:15,950 --> 00:41:19,230
to rank higher without requiring
a separate reward model.

825
00:41:19,230 --> 00:41:23,270
So this is a pretty
popular approach.

826
00:41:23,270 --> 00:41:26,830
And then humans,
though, humans are

827
00:41:26,830 --> 00:41:29,910
subjective and biased
as well as the fact

828
00:41:29,910 --> 00:41:32,070
that they're typically costly.

829
00:41:32,070 --> 00:41:34,470
If you need a bunch
of humans to rank

830
00:41:34,470 --> 00:41:37,190
thousands or millions
of outputs, for example,

831
00:41:37,190 --> 00:41:38,690
that can be infeasible.

832
00:41:38,690 --> 00:41:42,330
So another work does
this with AI, actually.

833
00:41:42,330 --> 00:41:45,270
So it gets AI off the
shelf LLMs in order

834
00:41:45,270 --> 00:41:48,070
to give their
preferences for which

835
00:41:48,070 --> 00:41:50,370
answers they prefer over
others, for example,

836
00:41:50,370 --> 00:41:53,850
and uses this in order
to tune the outputs.

837
00:41:56,750 --> 00:41:59,950
And then DeepSeek came up with
GRPO or Group Relative Policy

838
00:41:59,950 --> 00:42:01,210
Optimization.

839
00:42:01,210 --> 00:42:04,790
So rather than simply binary
ranking responses of which one

840
00:42:04,790 --> 00:42:06,910
they prefer over the
other, this actually

841
00:42:06,910 --> 00:42:11,750
ranks responses in a group using
multiple different responses.

842
00:42:11,750 --> 00:42:14,647
This provides richer, more
fine-grained feedback,

843
00:42:14,647 --> 00:42:16,230
which has shown to
improve performance

844
00:42:16,230 --> 00:42:18,170
on many tasks such
as mathematics.

845
00:42:21,630 --> 00:42:24,390
And then the last part I
want to touch upon here

846
00:42:24,390 --> 00:42:27,710
for reinforcement learning is
process supervision or process

847
00:42:27,710 --> 00:42:28,490
rewards.

848
00:42:28,490 --> 00:42:31,070
So again, a lot of
problems or a lot

849
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:33,990
of tasks that you want a
language model, or robot,

850
00:42:33,990 --> 00:42:36,830
or whatever to solve
involve multiple steps.

851
00:42:36,830 --> 00:42:39,230
So rather than only having
a reward at the end,

852
00:42:39,230 --> 00:42:41,310
it might be more
optimal to actually give

853
00:42:41,310 --> 00:42:45,660
intermediate reward
signals to your model.

854
00:42:45,660 --> 00:42:48,040
So this is exactly what
process supervision is.

855
00:42:48,040 --> 00:42:51,240
So you label or evaluate
intermediate reasoning steps,

856
00:42:51,240 --> 00:42:52,660
and then you train
models in order

857
00:42:52,660 --> 00:42:55,560
to more effectively produce
good reasoning traces.

858
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:57,640
So the individual steps
are more accurate,

859
00:42:57,640 --> 00:43:01,940
which leads to, of course,
a better final answer

860
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:03,820
but also better
overall reasoning

861
00:43:03,820 --> 00:43:06,180
behavior and performance.

862
00:43:06,180 --> 00:43:08,540
And this also reduces
things like reward-hacking

863
00:43:08,540 --> 00:43:09,400
and so forth.

864
00:43:12,740 --> 00:43:15,620
Next, in a similar vein,
I'll talk about AI agents

865
00:43:15,620 --> 00:43:18,060
and specifically
self-improvement.

866
00:43:18,060 --> 00:43:19,560
So what is an AI agent?

867
00:43:19,560 --> 00:43:22,280
You've probably used one, Cloud
Code or that sort of thing.

868
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:24,840
So it's just a system that
perceives its environment,

869
00:43:24,840 --> 00:43:27,360
makes some of decisions,
like this chain of thought,

870
00:43:27,360 --> 00:43:30,260
and then takes actions towards
achieving the goal that

871
00:43:30,260 --> 00:43:32,660
is generally given by a
human, so like you telling

872
00:43:32,660 --> 00:43:34,160
Cloud Code to build an app.

873
00:43:37,860 --> 00:43:40,980
One big benefit of agents
is that they can reflect

874
00:43:40,980 --> 00:43:42,780
and improve upon themselves.

875
00:43:42,780 --> 00:43:45,620
So the loop looks
something like this.

876
00:43:45,620 --> 00:43:48,380
When given an input
query, the agent

877
00:43:48,380 --> 00:43:50,920
can gather all of its
inputs, the query,

878
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:53,680
any other environmental factors
that might need to consider,

879
00:43:53,680 --> 00:43:55,220
can reason, plan,
and decide what

880
00:43:55,220 --> 00:43:58,620
it wants to build or do,
take action, and then observe

881
00:43:58,620 --> 00:43:59,540
what happened.

882
00:43:59,540 --> 00:44:01,600
So if you've ever
used Cloud Code,

883
00:44:01,600 --> 00:44:03,780
you might find that
it runs the script

884
00:44:03,780 --> 00:44:07,180
and sees if it compiled
successfully before reflecting

885
00:44:07,180 --> 00:44:13,460
and if needed, repeating the
same loop to update its outputs.

886
00:44:13,460 --> 00:44:17,120
So in this way, models can
currently self-improve.

887
00:44:17,120 --> 00:44:18,620
They can reflect
on their own output

888
00:44:18,620 --> 00:44:21,560
and try to iteratively
improve those outputs.

889
00:44:27,780 --> 00:44:29,280
Let's just skip that.

890
00:44:33,380 --> 00:44:37,380
So, yeah, models-- and this
way learn from past mistakes

891
00:44:37,380 --> 00:44:38,980
and adjust their
future responses

892
00:44:38,980 --> 00:44:40,980
based on their prior failures.

893
00:44:40,980 --> 00:44:45,230
Another thing that you can
do is have a memory store,

894
00:44:45,230 --> 00:44:47,890
so like GPT these days,
store some memory.

895
00:44:47,890 --> 00:44:51,410
And this way it can also
store its past mistakes

896
00:44:51,410 --> 00:44:54,730
and try to adjust the responses
that it gives you later based

897
00:44:54,730 --> 00:44:57,270
on what it learned
from those loops.

898
00:45:00,090 --> 00:45:04,330
And you can combine this as
well with tool use, API calls,

899
00:45:04,330 --> 00:45:06,970
or retrieval like we talked
about to further improve

900
00:45:06,970 --> 00:45:09,810
your agents so it can generate
a reasoning plan, which

901
00:45:09,810 --> 00:45:11,750
may involve calling
an external tool,

902
00:45:11,750 --> 00:45:13,970
running a web search,
database query,

903
00:45:13,970 --> 00:45:16,210
and use all of those
outputs, incorporate them

904
00:45:16,210 --> 00:45:17,710
into its final response.

905
00:45:20,850 --> 00:45:21,410
OK.

906
00:45:21,410 --> 00:45:23,730
So he talked a
lot about language

907
00:45:23,730 --> 00:45:25,613
on how all the
techniques that go

908
00:45:25,613 --> 00:45:27,530
into improving language
models and making them

909
00:45:27,530 --> 00:45:29,170
what they are today.

910
00:45:29,170 --> 00:45:32,850
Next, we'll move a bit onto
applications of transformers

911
00:45:32,850 --> 00:45:35,650
beyond language.

912
00:45:35,650 --> 00:45:39,610
So as we've all probably
seen, there are many,

913
00:45:39,610 --> 00:45:43,270
and AI has basically started
to take over every field.

914
00:45:43,270 --> 00:45:45,730
So there's things like
DeepMind's, AlphaFold,

915
00:45:45,730 --> 00:45:48,450
and genetics, lots
of medical models

916
00:45:48,450 --> 00:45:51,910
or medical language
models, AlphaGo for chess,

917
00:45:51,910 --> 00:45:54,770
et cetera, image generation.

918
00:45:54,770 --> 00:45:56,650
Speaking of image
generation, I'll

919
00:45:56,650 --> 00:46:01,330
talk really quickly about vision
transformers and how they work.

920
00:46:01,330 --> 00:46:05,130
So before vision
transformers, there

921
00:46:05,130 --> 00:46:09,210
were CNNs, Convolutional
Neural Networks.

922
00:46:09,210 --> 00:46:11,330
However, when they
came along and started

923
00:46:11,330 --> 00:46:13,850
yielding very good
performance on language,

924
00:46:13,850 --> 00:46:16,970
people started thinking about
ways to apply them to image.

925
00:46:16,970 --> 00:46:22,350
If you simply took every single
pixel and treated it as a token,

926
00:46:22,350 --> 00:46:25,310
even if you had a very
small 28 by 28 image,

927
00:46:25,310 --> 00:46:29,450
that would still flatten to
almost 1,000 pixels or tokens.

928
00:46:29,450 --> 00:46:32,250
And this is pretty expensive
even for today's hardware

929
00:46:32,250 --> 00:46:34,170
abilities.

930
00:46:34,170 --> 00:46:36,290
Hence, this paper or
vision transformer

931
00:46:36,290 --> 00:46:40,330
suggested breaking down
the images into patches

932
00:46:40,330 --> 00:46:43,890
and coding these patches
into smaller sequences

933
00:46:43,890 --> 00:46:46,090
that a transformer
could learn from.

934
00:46:46,090 --> 00:46:49,370
And this worked very
well for various tasks,

935
00:46:49,370 --> 00:46:52,650
like I touched on towards
the beginning, served

936
00:46:52,650 --> 00:46:55,350
as a very good representation
learning algorithm as well.

937
00:46:57,930 --> 00:47:02,090
However, if CNNs work so well
and work so well for many years,

938
00:47:02,090 --> 00:47:04,198
why use transformers?

939
00:47:04,198 --> 00:47:04,990
A couple of things.

940
00:47:04,990 --> 00:47:07,170
Transformers are very
flexible architectures

941
00:47:07,170 --> 00:47:09,450
with minimal inductive
priors, so they

942
00:47:09,450 --> 00:47:12,010
make very few assumptions
about the input data.

943
00:47:12,010 --> 00:47:15,850
In contrast, CNNs assume
that nearby pixels

944
00:47:15,850 --> 00:47:21,810
are related on locality in
that they process images

945
00:47:21,810 --> 00:47:22,910
with filters.

946
00:47:22,910 --> 00:47:24,890
So you might have a
4 by 4 filter, which

947
00:47:24,890 --> 00:47:32,010
is assuming that, that scale
has relations inside of it.

948
00:47:32,010 --> 00:47:35,730
These assumptions help
CNNs learn effectively

949
00:47:35,730 --> 00:47:37,800
with limited training
data, however, also

950
00:47:37,800 --> 00:47:39,440
bottlenecked their performance.

951
00:47:39,440 --> 00:47:42,000
Transformers, on
the other hand, have

952
00:47:42,000 --> 00:47:44,260
to learn from very
large data sets,

953
00:47:44,260 --> 00:47:47,640
so you can't really train
on the same scale as CNNs.

954
00:47:47,640 --> 00:47:50,480
However, they make
no assumptions

955
00:47:50,480 --> 00:47:54,160
and can therefore learn much
better on these larger data

956
00:47:54,160 --> 00:47:54,700
sets.

957
00:47:57,600 --> 00:48:01,640
Another big shift in
the field was CLIP.

958
00:48:01,640 --> 00:48:03,960
So trying to align
text representations

959
00:48:03,960 --> 00:48:06,000
with image representations.

960
00:48:06,000 --> 00:48:08,320
And they did this pretty
simply and intuitively

961
00:48:08,320 --> 00:48:10,980
in that you encode your
text into a vector,

962
00:48:10,980 --> 00:48:14,120
you encode your image into a
vector through separate models,

963
00:48:14,120 --> 00:48:15,800
and then update
these models to try

964
00:48:15,800 --> 00:48:23,320
to align both through paired
data for images and text.

965
00:48:23,320 --> 00:48:26,240
So one application that
this thing that I work on

966
00:48:26,240 --> 00:48:30,640
is in neuroscience,
transformers for neuroscience.

967
00:48:30,640 --> 00:48:36,280
So with fMRI, we can capture
changes in blood oxygenation

968
00:48:36,280 --> 00:48:37,640
across the brain.

969
00:48:37,640 --> 00:48:40,240
However, these are very
noisy signals and also

970
00:48:40,240 --> 00:48:43,960
very high dimensional
and more so correlated

971
00:48:43,960 --> 00:48:48,360
fluctuations than really robust
activations, like a deep brain

972
00:48:48,360 --> 00:48:49,740
electrode might give you.

973
00:48:49,740 --> 00:48:51,320
Therefore, what
generally matters

974
00:48:51,320 --> 00:48:54,500
isn't the absolute
values of the signals,

975
00:48:54,500 --> 00:48:57,880
but rather the correlations
in between them.

976
00:48:57,880 --> 00:49:00,880
So one line of
work in this field

977
00:49:00,880 --> 00:49:06,560
was to try to cluster the
different parts of the brain

978
00:49:06,560 --> 00:49:08,840
into networks.

979
00:49:08,840 --> 00:49:12,160
And they found that
certain numbers of networks

980
00:49:12,160 --> 00:49:15,720
were more stable than others,
and they settled on 7 and 17.

981
00:49:15,720 --> 00:49:19,480
So if we decompose your brain
into seven broad networks,

982
00:49:19,480 --> 00:49:22,360
we get a hierarchy that looks
like your visual center, so

983
00:49:22,360 --> 00:49:25,840
processing vision,
things like salience,

984
00:49:25,840 --> 00:49:29,680
which regulates attention
and your senses,

985
00:49:29,680 --> 00:49:31,740
and the DMN, which
is like daydreaming.

986
00:49:34,990 --> 00:49:39,893
So if you want to apply
transformers to this data,

987
00:49:39,893 --> 00:49:41,310
one thing you could
do very simply

988
00:49:41,310 --> 00:49:44,270
is to just train with
random masking on the data.

989
00:49:44,270 --> 00:49:46,530
And this works OK
at large scales.

990
00:49:46,530 --> 00:49:49,590
However, we wanted to make
a more interpretable better

991
00:49:49,590 --> 00:49:50,650
architecture.

992
00:49:50,650 --> 00:49:52,870
So we took this notion
of dividing the brain up

993
00:49:52,870 --> 00:49:56,150
into networks and gave this
to the model as a prior.

994
00:49:56,150 --> 00:49:59,750
So instead of predicting
random patches masked out

995
00:49:59,750 --> 00:50:02,930
throughout the brain, we
mask out an entire network,

996
00:50:02,930 --> 00:50:05,610
say, all the information
in your visual center,

997
00:50:05,610 --> 00:50:08,190
and see if the model can predict
that given the information

998
00:50:08,190 --> 00:50:10,270
in the rest of your brain.

999
00:50:10,270 --> 00:50:14,030
This allows us to learn
more robust embeddings that

1000
00:50:14,030 --> 00:50:17,430
give us insights into diseases,
like Alzheimer's disease.

1001
00:50:17,430 --> 00:50:19,750
So on the left,
bottom left, you can

1002
00:50:19,750 --> 00:50:23,550
see that the embeddings
trained from this model cluster

1003
00:50:23,550 --> 00:50:26,470
based on disease
status, so CNS control--

1004
00:50:26,470 --> 00:50:28,650
MCI is Mild Cognitive
Impairment--

1005
00:50:28,650 --> 00:50:31,390
which eventually generally
leads in old age to Alzheimer's

1006
00:50:31,390 --> 00:50:33,870
disease if one is prone to it.

1007
00:50:33,870 --> 00:50:35,930
Additionally, with
this architecture,

1008
00:50:35,930 --> 00:50:39,150
we can probe dependencies
between different parts

1009
00:50:39,150 --> 00:50:39,890
of the brain.

1010
00:50:39,890 --> 00:50:43,050
So how connected is
your visual system, say,

1011
00:50:43,050 --> 00:50:46,750
your daydreaming network?

1012
00:50:46,750 --> 00:50:48,750
And with this, we
get pretty plots

1013
00:50:48,750 --> 00:50:51,110
like this, which show
us all the connections

1014
00:50:51,110 --> 00:50:53,870
in between different parts
of the brain, and also

1015
00:50:53,870 --> 00:50:57,030
how predictable each
network is based

1016
00:50:57,030 --> 00:50:58,870
on the information in
the rest of the brain.

1017
00:50:58,870 --> 00:51:01,110
So we see that
your visual center

1018
00:51:01,110 --> 00:51:03,550
is pretty predictable from
the rest of your brain,

1019
00:51:03,550 --> 00:51:07,430
whereas others, like subcortical
regions involved in memory

1020
00:51:07,430 --> 00:51:08,250
are more separate.

1021
00:51:08,250 --> 00:51:11,590
You can't really predict them
from the rest of the brain.

1022
00:51:11,590 --> 00:51:14,630
Also since we have data from
multiple different groups,

1023
00:51:14,630 --> 00:51:17,070
including those with
Alzheimer's disease,

1024
00:51:17,070 --> 00:51:21,230
we can see where changes
happen inside the brain.

1025
00:51:21,230 --> 00:51:24,350
So we find that the DNN
or Daydreaming Network

1026
00:51:24,350 --> 00:51:26,190
is disproportionately
affected compared

1027
00:51:26,190 --> 00:51:27,370
to the rest of the brain.

1028
00:51:27,370 --> 00:51:32,570
So this might help influence,
say, drug development

1029
00:51:32,570 --> 00:51:35,630
or give us more mechanistic
knowledge into how

1030
00:51:35,630 --> 00:51:37,010
the disease progresses.

1031
00:51:39,990 --> 00:51:42,190
And if you find this
work interesting,

1032
00:51:42,190 --> 00:51:44,910
can read the paper as well.

1033
00:51:44,910 --> 00:51:47,350
So a very different
application of transformers

1034
00:51:47,350 --> 00:51:48,650
to medical imaging.

1035
00:51:54,190 --> 00:51:57,110
So the future, what's next?

1036
00:51:57,110 --> 00:52:01,150
We've seen that transformers
have pretty much pervaded

1037
00:52:01,150 --> 00:52:03,710
into all of our lives.

1038
00:52:03,710 --> 00:52:07,850
So however, they can still
label a lot more applications.

1039
00:52:07,850 --> 00:52:11,510
So increasingly, we have domain
specific foundation models

1040
00:52:11,510 --> 00:52:17,550
and medicine and law, cetera,
and also increasingly getting

1041
00:52:17,550 --> 00:52:21,150
integrated into the real world
with personalized education

1042
00:52:21,150 --> 00:52:23,890
in some countries,
advancing health,

1043
00:52:23,890 --> 00:52:28,740
taking load off doctors,
and more fun applications

1044
00:52:28,740 --> 00:52:32,420
as well, like interactive
entertainment and gaming,

1045
00:52:32,420 --> 00:52:37,540
having your NPC talk
to you as an AI.

1046
00:52:37,540 --> 00:52:40,700
However, there are still many
missing ingredients to AGI

1047
00:52:40,700 --> 00:52:43,780
or what we're all chasing.

1048
00:52:43,780 --> 00:52:45,360
A few are memory.

1049
00:52:45,360 --> 00:52:47,700
So models right now still
have very limited memory

1050
00:52:47,700 --> 00:52:50,260
unlike humans, which
have fast recall

1051
00:52:50,260 --> 00:52:55,260
and can access
very large stores.

1052
00:52:55,260 --> 00:52:57,780
So to fix these
things, we also have

1053
00:52:57,780 --> 00:53:00,780
to reduce computational
complexity.

1054
00:53:00,780 --> 00:53:02,380
It's not very
sustainable to train

1055
00:53:02,380 --> 00:53:06,340
on trillions of tokens with
trillions of parameters,

1056
00:53:06,340 --> 00:53:09,940
and also things like allowing
models to self-improve further

1057
00:53:09,940 --> 00:53:11,640
beyond what agents can do.

1058
00:53:11,640 --> 00:53:13,860
So actually update
their parameters

1059
00:53:13,860 --> 00:53:16,020
and get better at
tasks as they come

1060
00:53:16,020 --> 00:53:19,500
across them, more
autonomy and long horizon

1061
00:53:19,500 --> 00:53:21,580
decision making as well.

1062
00:53:21,580 --> 00:53:23,980
And throughout all of
this, we have to make sure

1063
00:53:23,980 --> 00:53:26,380
to try to align them with
emotional intelligence

1064
00:53:26,380 --> 00:53:30,340
and social understanding,
align them with our values

1065
00:53:30,340 --> 00:53:34,500
so they don't treat
us as adversaries.

1066
00:53:34,500 --> 00:53:38,660
There is also a direction of
minifying models on device LLMs,

1067
00:53:38,660 --> 00:53:39,940
like we talked about.

1068
00:53:39,940 --> 00:53:42,920
So if we can make smaller
models much better,

1069
00:53:42,920 --> 00:53:44,420
we can run them
on, say, our phones

1070
00:53:44,420 --> 00:53:48,540
and have similar performance or
help us more with daily tasks.

1071
00:53:48,540 --> 00:53:52,180
And in the future, we may even
be able to fine tune models

1072
00:53:52,180 --> 00:53:52,880
locally.

1073
00:53:56,820 --> 00:53:59,940
And in a similar vein, there
are many limits to scaling.

1074
00:53:59,940 --> 00:54:02,780
So going up in data
size or model size,

1075
00:54:02,780 --> 00:54:07,060
already there are diminishing
returns from scaling alone.

1076
00:54:07,060 --> 00:54:10,580
Training on web
data, as we do today,

1077
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:12,420
probably won't get us to AGI.

1078
00:54:12,420 --> 00:54:15,300
So there are shifts to more
post-training methods like we

1079
00:54:15,300 --> 00:54:20,440
talked about, RLHF or RLAIF,
improving data quality,

1080
00:54:20,440 --> 00:54:23,740
so curriculums, maybe trying
to see how humans learn

1081
00:54:23,740 --> 00:54:26,832
and Steven's work.

1082
00:54:26,832 --> 00:54:29,290
However, another risk of all
these post-training techniques

1083
00:54:29,290 --> 00:54:32,610
like RLHF is that if
you train the model

1084
00:54:32,610 --> 00:54:34,730
too much in
post-training, it might

1085
00:54:34,730 --> 00:54:36,610
forget what it learned
in pre-training

1086
00:54:36,610 --> 00:54:38,930
and get worse on the
task rather than better.

1087
00:54:38,930 --> 00:54:42,810
So still ongoing work to try to
break through this scaling law

1088
00:54:42,810 --> 00:54:45,090
limit.

1089
00:54:45,090 --> 00:54:47,930
And this might happen through
new architectures, higher

1090
00:54:47,930 --> 00:54:53,890
quality data, improve
training procedures,

1091
00:54:53,890 --> 00:54:58,130
and like I mentioned,
also making

1092
00:54:58,130 --> 00:55:01,370
smaller models more advanced.

1093
00:55:01,370 --> 00:55:04,170
Yeah.

1094
00:55:04,170 --> 00:55:05,610
Great.

1095
00:55:05,610 --> 00:55:07,890
So there's still a lot
of remaining weaknesses

1096
00:55:07,890 --> 00:55:08,710
or challenges.

1097
00:55:08,710 --> 00:55:11,870
So Karan touched upon
reaching limits with scaling.

1098
00:55:11,870 --> 00:55:14,090
I'm trying to get these
models more computationally

1099
00:55:14,090 --> 00:55:15,917
efficient so we can
run them on our phone.

1100
00:55:15,917 --> 00:55:18,250
We can maybe even train them
on our phones in the future

1101
00:55:18,250 --> 00:55:19,610
and so forth.

1102
00:55:19,610 --> 00:55:23,530
Another big issue that remains,
especially in language models,

1103
00:55:23,530 --> 00:55:28,010
but models in general, it's
this thing called hallucination.

1104
00:55:28,010 --> 00:55:29,810
So what is hallucination?

1105
00:55:29,810 --> 00:55:32,650
We typically think
of this as when

1106
00:55:32,650 --> 00:55:35,450
a language model, for example,
provides false or incorrect

1107
00:55:35,450 --> 00:55:39,190
information, it'll make up
things, facts, citations,

1108
00:55:39,190 --> 00:55:42,050
details that don't exist,
but also usually sound

1109
00:55:42,050 --> 00:55:44,130
quite confident about it.

1110
00:55:44,130 --> 00:55:47,672
It fails to reflect uncertainty
when the knowledge is missing.

1111
00:55:47,672 --> 00:55:49,130
It doesn't really
know what to say.

1112
00:55:49,130 --> 00:55:49,672
I don't know.

1113
00:55:49,672 --> 00:55:54,930
But it tries to always--
it pretends like it

1114
00:55:54,930 --> 00:55:56,530
knows everything, basically.

1115
00:55:56,530 --> 00:56:00,330
So why this happens, there's
a few potential explanations.

1116
00:56:00,330 --> 00:56:04,770
One is models optimized for
plausibility and not truth.

1117
00:56:04,770 --> 00:56:07,850
They're trained to predict
or produce the next token

1118
00:56:07,850 --> 00:56:09,810
with the highest probability.

1119
00:56:09,810 --> 00:56:11,750
So this is not
necessarily true text.

1120
00:56:11,750 --> 00:56:13,850
It's just the most
probable text.

1121
00:56:13,850 --> 00:56:16,530
There's also a
lack of grounding.

1122
00:56:16,530 --> 00:56:20,130
So this relates to what I'll
talk about later, world models.

1123
00:56:20,130 --> 00:56:23,010
But language models,
they don't really

1124
00:56:23,010 --> 00:56:28,370
have notion of the world
or external grounding.

1125
00:56:28,370 --> 00:56:31,490
And further, a lot of the times
when users talk to models,

1126
00:56:31,490 --> 00:56:33,130
they're ambiguous
or they give prompts

1127
00:56:33,130 --> 00:56:35,030
that don't have
enough information.

1128
00:56:35,030 --> 00:56:37,290
So then this can
confuse the model,

1129
00:56:37,290 --> 00:56:40,450
for example, which
prompts it to then produce

1130
00:56:40,450 --> 00:56:45,090
hallucinated information.

1131
00:56:45,090 --> 00:56:47,130
So again, hallucination
reduces things

1132
00:56:47,130 --> 00:56:48,850
like trust and reliability.

1133
00:56:48,850 --> 00:56:53,150
And especially in high stakes
domains like law and health

1134
00:56:53,150 --> 00:56:55,330
care, you don't want to
rely on a model that's

1135
00:56:55,330 --> 00:56:58,830
just saying whatever,
things that are incorrect.

1136
00:56:58,830 --> 00:57:01,050
And furthermore, a lot of
times it's hard to detect.

1137
00:57:01,050 --> 00:57:03,290
Again, because the
model sounds confident,

1138
00:57:03,290 --> 00:57:07,210
so how can you be sure if
it's hallucinating or not?

1139
00:57:07,210 --> 00:57:11,130
Furthermore, not all errors are
hallucinations, and what is true

1140
00:57:11,130 --> 00:57:13,530
depends on the context as well.

1141
00:57:13,530 --> 00:57:16,408
So these are things
I'll touch upon.

1142
00:57:16,408 --> 00:57:17,950
But overall, there's
a thing missing.

1143
00:57:17,950 --> 00:57:20,520
What exactly is a hallucination?

1144
00:57:20,520 --> 00:57:23,960
We still don't have a concrete
or unified definition.

1145
00:57:23,960 --> 00:57:27,660
So this is one of our
recent works looks at,

1146
00:57:27,660 --> 00:57:30,560
which is we actually
define a unified definition

1147
00:57:30,560 --> 00:57:32,960
of hallucination.

1148
00:57:32,960 --> 00:57:35,620
So again, existing
definitions are different.

1149
00:57:35,620 --> 00:57:38,720
It depends on things like the
field or the task at hand.

1150
00:57:38,720 --> 00:57:41,200
For example, in
summarization, a hallucination

1151
00:57:41,200 --> 00:57:44,200
means when the language model
produces an output which is not

1152
00:57:44,200 --> 00:57:48,640
faithful to the source document,
whereas an open domain QA,

1153
00:57:48,640 --> 00:57:52,000
it means it produces a factually
incorrect response, something

1154
00:57:52,000 --> 00:57:54,480
that is not true
in the real world.

1155
00:57:54,480 --> 00:57:56,640
And in the case of
agents, hallucination

1156
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:59,480
means, for example, when it
might take incorrect actions

1157
00:57:59,480 --> 00:58:01,680
based on, for
example, hallucinating

1158
00:58:01,680 --> 00:58:04,920
a button on the web page that
doesn't exist and so forth.

1159
00:58:04,920 --> 00:58:08,680
So the same output can be a
hallucination in one setting,

1160
00:58:08,680 --> 00:58:11,360
but correct in another.

1161
00:58:11,360 --> 00:58:14,200
So our core idea is that
hallucination is basically

1162
00:58:14,200 --> 00:58:15,720
about world models.

1163
00:58:15,720 --> 00:58:18,280
So your large language
model or whatever model

1164
00:58:18,280 --> 00:58:20,520
has learned a
internal world model

1165
00:58:20,520 --> 00:58:22,400
through things like
pre-training or training

1166
00:58:22,400 --> 00:58:23,840
on large amounts of data.

1167
00:58:23,840 --> 00:58:26,060
On the other hand, when
you have a task at hand,

1168
00:58:26,060 --> 00:58:29,343
you have an external or ground
truth reference world model.

1169
00:58:29,343 --> 00:58:30,760
For example, this
is a world model

1170
00:58:30,760 --> 00:58:33,280
that is described by
your source document

1171
00:58:33,280 --> 00:58:35,160
when it comes to summarization.

1172
00:58:35,160 --> 00:58:37,220
And this world model,
on the other hand,

1173
00:58:37,220 --> 00:58:41,480
is the real world itself when it
comes to things open domain QA.

1174
00:58:41,480 --> 00:58:44,200
So this world that the
language model learned

1175
00:58:44,200 --> 00:58:48,120
may match this reference world
model or may contradict it.

1176
00:58:48,120 --> 00:58:53,280
So hallucination occurs when
the model's learned world model

1177
00:58:53,280 --> 00:58:57,600
is it contradicts what is the
reference or the ground truth

1178
00:58:57,600 --> 00:58:58,560
world model.

1179
00:58:58,560 --> 00:59:01,560
So it's not just about
producing a wrong answer,

1180
00:59:01,560 --> 00:59:04,920
but having wrong beliefs
about the world itself.

1181
00:59:04,920 --> 00:59:06,600
So in general, we
define hallucination

1182
00:59:06,600 --> 00:59:10,000
as a world modeling error.

1183
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:12,840
So we break this down into
structured mathematical

1184
00:59:12,840 --> 00:59:13,600
framework.

1185
00:59:13,600 --> 00:59:16,390
So every hallucination
depends on three things.

1186
00:59:16,390 --> 00:59:19,030
Like I said, a reference
world model which defines what

1187
00:59:19,030 --> 00:59:20,870
is actually true.

1188
00:59:20,870 --> 00:59:23,170
For example, this can be
a document, a database,

1189
00:59:23,170 --> 00:59:24,870
the real world, and
so forth, depending

1190
00:59:24,870 --> 00:59:27,110
on the context and situation.

1191
00:59:27,110 --> 00:59:28,170
You also have a view.

1192
00:59:28,170 --> 00:59:30,270
So this is what the
model can actually see.

1193
00:59:30,270 --> 00:59:33,030
For example, information that
it has retrieved from a database

1194
00:59:33,030 --> 00:59:34,350
is part of its view.

1195
00:59:34,350 --> 00:59:36,630
And then you have
a conflict policy.

1196
00:59:36,630 --> 00:59:38,750
So when you have different
sources of information

1197
00:59:38,750 --> 00:59:42,567
that may disagree, you need
a way of resolving conflicts.

1198
00:59:42,567 --> 00:59:44,150
For example, we know
in the real world

1199
00:59:44,150 --> 00:59:45,630
that Harry Potter is a boy.

1200
00:59:45,630 --> 00:59:48,590
But, for example, in
a specific situation,

1201
00:59:48,590 --> 00:59:50,910
a Harry Potter might be a girl.

1202
00:59:50,910 --> 00:59:53,150
And, for example, when
someone is role playing

1203
00:59:53,150 --> 00:59:55,290
or they're making
up their own story,

1204
00:59:55,290 --> 00:59:57,750
the user, so in
that case, the model

1205
00:59:57,750 --> 00:59:59,670
should align with
what the user thinks

1206
00:59:59,670 --> 01:00:02,990
is true, which is that
Harry Potter is a girl.

1207
01:00:02,990 --> 01:00:05,470
So you need a way of
resolving these conflicts

1208
01:00:05,470 --> 01:00:06,870
when sources disagree.

1209
01:00:06,870 --> 01:00:10,590
So a hallucination is when
a model outputs something

1210
01:00:10,590 --> 01:00:13,110
that contains a
claim that is false

1211
01:00:13,110 --> 01:00:16,090
given these three components.

1212
01:00:18,670 --> 01:00:22,190
So this is a unified
definition because we

1213
01:00:22,190 --> 01:00:24,350
found that pretty much
all existing hallucination

1214
01:00:24,350 --> 01:00:27,110
definitions and cases
of hallucination

1215
01:00:27,110 --> 01:00:29,590
and different tasks
and situations

1216
01:00:29,590 --> 01:00:32,390
can all be put under
this framework.

1217
01:00:32,390 --> 01:00:35,150
For example, in summarization,
again, our reference world

1218
01:00:35,150 --> 01:00:36,950
is the source document.

1219
01:00:36,950 --> 01:00:39,950
What the model sees, its
view is this document,

1220
01:00:39,950 --> 01:00:42,570
and our conflict policy is
the document is the truth.

1221
01:00:42,570 --> 01:00:46,210
It doesn't matter what is in
the real world and so forth,

1222
01:00:46,210 --> 01:00:48,550
but the document is
your source of truth.

1223
01:00:48,550 --> 01:00:52,270
And a hallucination type is
an intrinsic contradiction

1224
01:00:52,270 --> 01:00:54,190
where the language
model produces

1225
01:00:54,190 --> 01:00:56,790
an output, which contains
a claim which contradicts

1226
01:00:56,790 --> 01:00:58,270
the source document.

1227
01:00:58,270 --> 01:01:00,950
On the other hand, for
example, for agents, this

1228
01:01:00,950 --> 01:01:05,230
is when the environment, the web
page is exposed to is the truth,

1229
01:01:05,230 --> 01:01:07,895
and the agent
hallucinates in that it

1230
01:01:07,895 --> 01:01:10,270
thinks its environment is
different from what it actually

1231
01:01:10,270 --> 01:01:10,810
is.

1232
01:01:10,810 --> 01:01:15,060
So our unified definition
allows for this encompassing

1233
01:01:15,060 --> 01:01:17,700
of all the prior different
definitions and domain

1234
01:01:17,700 --> 01:01:21,060
and tasks.

1235
01:01:21,060 --> 01:01:23,800
This also allows us to
separate different errors.

1236
01:01:23,800 --> 01:01:27,180
Having a grounded
definition like ours--

1237
01:01:27,180 --> 01:01:30,300
for example, hallucination means
a wrong belief about the world--

1238
01:01:30,300 --> 01:01:32,540
separates it from things
like planning errors

1239
01:01:32,540 --> 01:01:35,280
by agents, which is when
its beliefs are right,

1240
01:01:35,280 --> 01:01:36,640
but it takes the wrong action.

1241
01:01:36,640 --> 01:01:38,280
So there's a clear
difference there,

1242
01:01:38,280 --> 01:01:41,140
which is important
to distinguish.

1243
01:01:41,140 --> 01:01:43,740
For example, like I said,
clicking a non-existent button

1244
01:01:43,740 --> 01:01:46,860
is hallucination, whereas
choosing a bad strategy

1245
01:01:46,860 --> 01:01:48,120
is a planning error.

1246
01:01:48,120 --> 01:01:51,020
So the takeaway is that
hallucination is not the same

1247
01:01:51,020 --> 01:01:54,940
as all types of model errors.

1248
01:01:54,940 --> 01:01:57,060
So why this definition
matters is that it

1249
01:01:57,060 --> 01:01:59,420
makes assumptions explicit.

1250
01:01:59,420 --> 01:02:02,220
So in the future, when people
talk about hallucination

1251
01:02:02,220 --> 01:02:05,540
or investigate things like
hallucination mitigation

1252
01:02:05,540 --> 01:02:08,860
techniques, they should define
what is the reference or ground

1253
01:02:08,860 --> 01:02:11,520
truth world model, what is
the view that the model sees,

1254
01:02:11,520 --> 01:02:14,460
and what is the conflict
resolution policy.

1255
01:02:14,460 --> 01:02:20,000
So this allows for better
comparisons across benchmarks,

1256
01:02:20,000 --> 01:02:23,140
consistent definitions,
and allows for assumptions

1257
01:02:23,140 --> 01:02:24,720
to be explicitly stated.

1258
01:02:27,380 --> 01:02:30,820
Furthermore, this also
enables the production

1259
01:02:30,820 --> 01:02:32,440
of scalable benchmarks.

1260
01:02:32,440 --> 01:02:34,300
So we're currently
working on an extension

1261
01:02:34,300 --> 01:02:36,260
to this paper,
which is producing

1262
01:02:36,260 --> 01:02:38,080
a benchmark of HalluWorld.

1263
01:02:38,080 --> 01:02:40,980
So by having a mathematical
grounded definition, what

1264
01:02:40,980 --> 01:02:46,180
it means is you can produce a
lot of examples of hallucination

1265
01:02:46,180 --> 01:02:49,780
through synthetic environments
by manipulating those three

1266
01:02:49,780 --> 01:02:51,340
different aspects
I talked about,

1267
01:02:51,340 --> 01:02:53,048
the reference world,
the view, as well as

1268
01:02:53,048 --> 01:02:54,920
the conflict resolution policy.

1269
01:02:54,920 --> 01:02:57,580
So again, having them
be explicitly defined

1270
01:02:57,580 --> 01:03:00,420
means you can mathematically
manipulate them

1271
01:03:00,420 --> 01:03:03,020
and as a result
construct a large scale

1272
01:03:03,020 --> 01:03:07,000
variety of different synthetic
hallucination examples.

1273
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:10,200
So this can be used for
evaluating hallucination models,

1274
01:03:10,200 --> 01:03:12,820
as well as for training better
hallucination mitigation

1275
01:03:12,820 --> 01:03:15,500
techniques in the future.

1276
01:03:15,500 --> 01:03:17,220
So feel free to
check out our paper,

1277
01:03:17,220 --> 01:03:22,380
and our benchmark will
be released later on.

1278
01:03:22,380 --> 01:03:24,540
So in general, again,
we define hallucination

1279
01:03:24,540 --> 01:03:26,940
as incorrect world
modeling, which depends on

1280
01:03:26,940 --> 01:03:29,740
what is true, the ground
truth reference world, what

1281
01:03:29,740 --> 01:03:31,900
is seen by the model,
and how conflict should

1282
01:03:31,900 --> 01:03:33,620
be resolved by the model.

1283
01:03:33,620 --> 01:03:35,260
And the key
implications again, are

1284
01:03:35,260 --> 01:03:38,340
to reduce confusion
across papers

1285
01:03:38,340 --> 01:03:40,860
by making assumptions
explicit and allowing

1286
01:03:40,860 --> 01:03:45,980
for better evaluation benchmarks
and mitigation techniques.

1287
01:03:45,980 --> 01:03:50,220
Next, so we talked a lot--

1288
01:03:50,220 --> 01:03:52,100
well, Kart mentioned
briefly about things

1289
01:03:52,100 --> 01:03:55,420
like continual learning.

1290
01:03:55,420 --> 01:03:59,020
So I'll talk first
about long-term memory.

1291
01:03:59,020 --> 01:04:01,820
So a lot of current models
are still stateless,

1292
01:04:01,820 --> 01:04:04,820
as in there's no persistent
learning across interactions.

1293
01:04:04,820 --> 01:04:07,650
So what we want is some
way of storing information

1294
01:04:07,650 --> 01:04:08,750
across sessions.

1295
01:04:08,750 --> 01:04:11,070
Like the human brain, we're
able to remember things.

1296
01:04:11,070 --> 01:04:14,210
We have a memory system
built into our heads.

1297
01:04:14,210 --> 01:04:17,502
This allows us to retrieve
and reuse past experiences.

1298
01:04:17,502 --> 01:04:19,210
And we want a model
to be able to do that

1299
01:04:19,210 --> 01:04:21,690
as well to update its
knowledge over time.

1300
01:04:21,690 --> 01:04:24,530
This will allow it to
personalize and adapt

1301
01:04:24,530 --> 01:04:27,882
to different situations
as they change,

1302
01:04:27,882 --> 01:04:30,090
and as well as support things
like continual learning

1303
01:04:30,090 --> 01:04:35,250
and long horizon
reasoning and planning.

1304
01:04:35,250 --> 01:04:37,850
So today, a lot of
memory is stored

1305
01:04:37,850 --> 01:04:41,530
in things like external systems,
things like vector databases

1306
01:04:41,530 --> 01:04:43,610
and so forth that
we retrieve upon.

1307
01:04:43,610 --> 01:04:46,370
Sometimes what we do
is we simply summarize

1308
01:04:46,370 --> 01:04:51,650
past information and keep it as
compressed parts of the context.

1309
01:04:51,650 --> 01:04:54,670
We also use structured
memory, data structures,

1310
01:04:54,670 --> 01:04:56,890
things like lists,
graphs, and so forth.

1311
01:04:56,890 --> 01:05:01,330
And what is missing is more
reliable memory updating.

1312
01:05:01,330 --> 01:05:03,450
So when we have new
information that we

1313
01:05:03,450 --> 01:05:05,970
want to store into memory,
what if it contradicts

1314
01:05:05,970 --> 01:05:07,230
previous information?

1315
01:05:07,230 --> 01:05:07,990
What happens then?

1316
01:05:07,990 --> 01:05:10,410
Do we erase the
previous-- do we override

1317
01:05:10,410 --> 01:05:11,750
the previous information?

1318
01:05:11,750 --> 01:05:14,870
Do we store them as two
separate pieces of information

1319
01:05:14,870 --> 01:05:15,750
and so forth?

1320
01:05:15,750 --> 01:05:18,990
And when our memory gets
super long and large,

1321
01:05:18,990 --> 01:05:24,790
that is overwhelming, how do we
prune or get rid of past memory?

1322
01:05:24,790 --> 01:05:26,470
Which examples do we throw out?

1323
01:05:26,470 --> 01:05:28,630
The oldest ones, the ones
that are least important?

1324
01:05:28,630 --> 01:05:33,472
How do we define which ones are
most important and so forth?

1325
01:05:33,472 --> 01:05:34,930
So this relates to
something called

1326
01:05:34,930 --> 01:05:37,490
lifelong or continuous learning.

1327
01:05:37,490 --> 01:05:42,370
So ideally, we want AI
systems that can continuously

1328
01:05:42,370 --> 01:05:44,307
learn after deployment.

1329
01:05:44,307 --> 01:05:45,890
So after they're
trained, when they're

1330
01:05:45,890 --> 01:05:48,473
deployed into real world, when
they're interacting with users,

1331
01:05:48,473 --> 01:05:50,490
we want them to
learn, for example,

1332
01:05:50,490 --> 01:05:53,210
through implicit feedback
and real world interactions

1333
01:05:53,210 --> 01:05:54,810
and experiences.

1334
01:05:54,810 --> 01:05:58,290
And ideally, we want this to
be infinite and permanent.

1335
01:05:58,290 --> 01:06:00,890
We want it to update
the model weights, which

1336
01:06:00,890 --> 01:06:02,310
is analogous to the brain.

1337
01:06:02,310 --> 01:06:04,240
This is the brain of the model.

1338
01:06:04,240 --> 01:06:07,000
So a lot of the current
ways that models

1339
01:06:07,000 --> 01:06:12,500
learn at inference time is
it's inference time only.

1340
01:06:12,500 --> 01:06:14,460
It's context only, for example.

1341
01:06:14,460 --> 01:06:15,900
So this isn't permanent.

1342
01:06:15,900 --> 01:06:18,840
The brain or weights of
the model aren't updating.

1343
01:06:18,840 --> 01:06:21,860
This leads to the debate, which
I won't get too much into here,

1344
01:06:21,860 --> 01:06:24,680
which is do we truly
need parametric updates

1345
01:06:24,680 --> 01:06:28,640
to the weights of the model for
it to be continuously learning,

1346
01:06:28,640 --> 01:06:32,440
or is in-context
learning enough?

1347
01:06:32,440 --> 01:06:37,872
But I argue that true continual
learning should involve updates

1348
01:06:37,872 --> 01:06:39,580
to the brain or the
weights of the model?

1349
01:06:42,240 --> 01:06:44,240
And current work, a lot
of this involves things

1350
01:06:44,240 --> 01:06:47,400
like model distillation,
things, again, self-improvement

1351
01:06:47,400 --> 01:06:49,560
and reflection, which are
again inference only time

1352
01:06:49,560 --> 01:06:52,380
enhancements, not updating
the brain of the model.

1353
01:06:52,380 --> 01:06:54,040
So what mechanisms
or things do we

1354
01:06:54,040 --> 01:06:56,800
need to investigate to really
get to true, continual,

1355
01:06:56,800 --> 01:06:58,820
or lifelong learning
like humans?

1356
01:06:58,820 --> 01:07:01,520
Again as humans, I'm learning
continuously right now

1357
01:07:01,520 --> 01:07:03,960
just by talking to you,
giving this lecture.

1358
01:07:03,960 --> 01:07:06,320
I don't need to be sat
down in a chair every month

1359
01:07:06,320 --> 01:07:08,600
and have the whole
internet read to me.

1360
01:07:08,600 --> 01:07:10,440
I'm learning
continuously on the spot

1361
01:07:10,440 --> 01:07:13,280
without needing a
explicit fine tuning

1362
01:07:13,280 --> 01:07:17,040
process every once in a while.

1363
01:07:17,040 --> 01:07:20,120
So there's this concept
of model editing, which

1364
01:07:20,120 --> 01:07:22,260
tries to edit specific
nodes of the model,

1365
01:07:22,260 --> 01:07:24,685
for example, given new
facts or information.

1366
01:07:24,685 --> 01:07:26,060
They use this
thing, for example,

1367
01:07:26,060 --> 01:07:30,840
called Rank-One Model Editing
or ROME to modify model weights.

1368
01:07:30,840 --> 01:07:33,600
But this has a lot of
inherent weaknesses.

1369
01:07:33,600 --> 01:07:36,000
It really only works
for simple facts.

1370
01:07:36,000 --> 01:07:38,680
And furthermore, it cannot
easily propagate changes

1371
01:07:38,680 --> 01:07:41,080
to other related
or dependent facts.

1372
01:07:41,080 --> 01:07:43,980
For example, if I
update, for example,

1373
01:07:43,980 --> 01:07:47,460
saying Bob's dad is adjacent
to Bob's, that is Justin,

1374
01:07:47,460 --> 01:07:51,160
but Bob also has a
sister, the sister's dad

1375
01:07:51,160 --> 01:07:52,540
should also be updated.

1376
01:07:52,540 --> 01:07:55,320
But this propagation
isn't really possible

1377
01:07:55,320 --> 01:07:58,360
when it comes to model editing
because there could be hundreds,

1378
01:07:58,360 --> 01:08:02,140
for example, related facts
that must be updated,

1379
01:08:02,140 --> 01:08:05,120
which is not really possible
with this model editing

1380
01:08:05,120 --> 01:08:08,120
approach.

1381
01:08:08,120 --> 01:08:11,100
Now, other than continual
learning and lifelong learning,

1382
01:08:11,100 --> 01:08:13,280
which I think is a
key piece to AGI,

1383
01:08:13,280 --> 01:08:16,920
there's another disadvantage
or weakness of very large scale

1384
01:08:16,920 --> 01:08:18,920
models these days.

1385
01:08:18,920 --> 01:08:21,160
So millions or
billions or trillions

1386
01:08:21,160 --> 01:08:24,600
of parameters, language
models trained on tons of data

1387
01:08:24,600 --> 01:08:27,359
means they're effectively a
very large black boxes, which

1388
01:08:27,359 --> 01:08:29,600
are hard to understand
and interpret.

1389
01:08:29,600 --> 01:08:32,100
So there's a line of
work on interpretability,

1390
01:08:32,100 --> 01:08:35,080
which is trying to understand
exactly how these models learn

1391
01:08:35,080 --> 01:08:36,200
and think.

1392
01:08:36,200 --> 01:08:38,540
They understand their
internal representations,

1393
01:08:38,540 --> 01:08:40,800
what they truly think,
not just what they're

1394
01:08:40,800 --> 01:08:42,800
outputting or producing.

1395
01:08:42,800 --> 01:08:45,040
So mechanistic
interpretability has

1396
01:08:45,040 --> 01:08:48,560
a lot of techniques looking at
things like circuits as well

1397
01:08:48,560 --> 01:08:52,000
as features and related
parts of the model

1398
01:08:52,000 --> 01:08:55,760
to understand what exactly is
going on internally to unpack

1399
01:08:55,760 --> 01:08:58,990
this black box.

1400
01:08:58,990 --> 01:09:03,990
Furthermore, such large models
means they're very effective,

1401
01:09:03,990 --> 01:09:07,029
and they're going to be
used for many things that

1402
01:09:07,029 --> 01:09:08,890
interface with the real world.

1403
01:09:08,890 --> 01:09:11,270
But it's important
that they're aligned.

1404
01:09:11,270 --> 01:09:13,870
So they do what exactly
we intend them to do

1405
01:09:13,870 --> 01:09:15,109
and that they're safe.

1406
01:09:15,109 --> 01:09:17,170
So there's this
alignment problem,

1407
01:09:17,170 --> 01:09:21,029
especially as models get
very large and powerful.

1408
01:09:21,029 --> 01:09:23,790
There's a lot of issues
with existing models, which

1409
01:09:23,790 --> 01:09:27,250
is a lot of the times they take
shortcuts to achieve a goal,

1410
01:09:27,250 --> 01:09:29,149
and these shortcuts
might be unsafe or not

1411
01:09:29,149 --> 01:09:30,870
exactly what you want.

1412
01:09:30,870 --> 01:09:33,470
They might not be able to
generalize well to new domains

1413
01:09:33,470 --> 01:09:36,710
and settings, so they might
be aligned or trained well,

1414
01:09:36,710 --> 01:09:38,330
but deployed to a
different setting,

1415
01:09:38,330 --> 01:09:42,450
they might exhibit erratic or
unintended, unsafe behavior.

1416
01:09:45,029 --> 01:09:48,550
So some specific failure modes
are things reward-hacking.

1417
01:09:48,550 --> 01:09:50,470
So instead of optimizing
the true goal,

1418
01:09:50,470 --> 01:09:53,590
it finds some way
of just optimizing

1419
01:09:53,590 --> 01:09:58,670
the reward and, for example,
a way that is not good.

1420
01:09:58,670 --> 01:10:02,630
It could also learn to optimize
the hidden objectives which

1421
01:10:02,630 --> 01:10:05,910
might not be known to the
person training the model

1422
01:10:05,910 --> 01:10:07,407
or using the model.

1423
01:10:07,407 --> 01:10:09,990
Additionally, we talked a lot
about chain of thought reasoning

1424
01:10:09,990 --> 01:10:11,490
or long reasoning chains.

1425
01:10:11,490 --> 01:10:14,910
But how do we know that
the reasoning steps

1426
01:10:14,910 --> 01:10:17,470
that the model outputs
is truly what it

1427
01:10:17,470 --> 01:10:20,130
believes or is using to reason?

1428
01:10:20,130 --> 01:10:22,350
So this is called
a faithfulness.

1429
01:10:22,350 --> 01:10:24,830
So again, the explanations
that the model is producing

1430
01:10:24,830 --> 01:10:27,870
might be post-hoc
rather than what

1431
01:10:27,870 --> 01:10:31,070
is actually conditioned upon in
order to get the right answer.

1432
01:10:31,070 --> 01:10:32,750
So things like
causal interventions

1433
01:10:32,750 --> 01:10:36,190
are one way of actually
assessing faithfulness

1434
01:10:36,190 --> 01:10:37,550
of reasoning steps.

1435
01:10:37,550 --> 01:10:39,710
And like I said, there
might be distribution shifts

1436
01:10:39,710 --> 01:10:43,510
where behaviors might
change in new environments.

1437
01:10:43,510 --> 01:10:45,910
So some different
alignment techniques,

1438
01:10:45,910 --> 01:10:48,630
we touched upon these
human and AI feedback.

1439
01:10:48,630 --> 01:10:51,790
This allows the model to
learn from human preferences

1440
01:10:51,790 --> 01:10:53,730
to be safer and more aligned.

1441
01:10:53,730 --> 01:10:58,060
But again, things like RLHF
are prone to two failure

1442
01:10:58,060 --> 01:11:00,020
modes like reward-hacking.

1443
01:11:00,020 --> 01:11:02,380
So that's something
to investigate.

1444
01:11:02,380 --> 01:11:04,360
Again, I talked about
interpretability,

1445
01:11:04,360 --> 01:11:06,620
truly understand what
is going on, especially

1446
01:11:06,620 --> 01:11:08,220
internally within the model.

1447
01:11:08,220 --> 01:11:11,240
And I also mentioned
earlier process supervision.

1448
01:11:11,240 --> 01:11:13,300
So being able to
reward reasoning steps

1449
01:11:13,300 --> 01:11:18,100
and actually examine individual
steps of a model's reasoning

1450
01:11:18,100 --> 01:11:21,900
process is important, rather
than simply only looking

1451
01:11:21,900 --> 01:11:25,700
at its final
answers or outcomes.

1452
01:11:25,700 --> 01:11:30,240
Furthermore, so this idea of
constitutional AI, I believe,

1453
01:11:30,240 --> 01:11:31,820
was introduced by Anthropic.

1454
01:11:31,820 --> 01:11:35,700
So they actually give it a
constitution, a written document

1455
01:11:35,700 --> 01:11:38,260
with a lot of rules or
behavior or principles

1456
01:11:38,260 --> 01:11:40,080
to guide the model's behavior.

1457
01:11:40,080 --> 01:11:41,860
And they actually
use this in order

1458
01:11:41,860 --> 01:11:47,100
to post train the
model to get it to more

1459
01:11:47,100 --> 01:11:48,940
aligned with this constitution.

1460
01:11:48,940 --> 01:11:51,520
There's also this idea
of scalable oversight,

1461
01:11:51,520 --> 01:11:56,020
so having humans oversee
these models, which

1462
01:11:56,020 --> 01:11:58,700
might grow to become super
powerful and very large

1463
01:11:58,700 --> 01:12:01,800
in number is not feasible.

1464
01:12:01,800 --> 01:12:05,400
So what if we use models
to supervise other models?

1465
01:12:05,400 --> 01:12:08,220
So that's the idea of
scalable oversight.

1466
01:12:08,220 --> 01:12:10,860
And overall,
alignment, the goal is

1467
01:12:10,860 --> 01:12:13,260
to ensure models
pursue the right goals

1468
01:12:13,260 --> 01:12:19,220
and behave reliably under all
different types of conditions.

1469
01:12:19,220 --> 01:12:23,420
So lastly, I'll talk about
what comes after transformers.

1470
01:12:23,420 --> 01:12:27,100
So again, our course, again,
it's titled Transformers United.

1471
01:12:27,100 --> 01:12:28,780
But frankly, I think
it's a good idea

1472
01:12:28,780 --> 01:12:34,940
to think outside the box
to not all focus only

1473
01:12:34,940 --> 01:12:38,060
on transformers research, which
is what most people are doing

1474
01:12:38,060 --> 01:12:40,940
these days, but to think
about what else might

1475
01:12:40,940 --> 01:12:43,660
be interesting to investigate
because there's no guarantee

1476
01:12:43,660 --> 01:12:46,780
that transformers and
next token prediction

1477
01:12:46,780 --> 01:12:51,460
will lead to AGI or
superintelligence.

1478
01:12:51,460 --> 01:12:55,040
So again, transformers dominate,
but they have limitations,

1479
01:12:55,040 --> 01:12:58,260
things like inefficiencies,
especially scaling with larger

1480
01:12:58,260 --> 01:13:02,220
context and sequence
lengths, it is quadratic.

1481
01:13:02,220 --> 01:13:05,580
It fails a lot of times
with long context reasoning,

1482
01:13:05,580 --> 01:13:08,100
and it doesn't
really truly learn--

1483
01:13:08,100 --> 01:13:09,620
well, this is debatable.

1484
01:13:09,620 --> 01:13:11,900
But I believe as
well that it has

1485
01:13:11,900 --> 01:13:14,520
limitations in terms of truly
understanding the world.

1486
01:13:14,520 --> 01:13:17,340
It's not truly learning
an effective world model

1487
01:13:17,340 --> 01:13:18,520
in most cases.

1488
01:13:18,520 --> 01:13:22,180
So this leads to these
two emerging directions.

1489
01:13:22,180 --> 01:13:24,620
So the first are world models
and this thing proposed

1490
01:13:24,620 --> 01:13:26,940
by Yann LeCun called JEPA.

1491
01:13:26,940 --> 01:13:29,140
It moves beyond next
token prediction

1492
01:13:29,140 --> 01:13:33,820
to actually truly try to
learn how the world works.

1493
01:13:33,820 --> 01:13:36,700
So world models are basically
structured representations

1494
01:13:36,700 --> 01:13:39,860
of environments, again,
the actual world,

1495
01:13:39,860 --> 01:13:44,120
and it's about predicting
future states, not just tokens.

1496
01:13:44,120 --> 01:13:46,900
And this will enable things
like planning and reasoning

1497
01:13:46,900 --> 01:13:49,700
more effectively over
time that is also grounded

1498
01:13:49,700 --> 01:13:52,330
in how the actual world works.

1499
01:13:52,330 --> 01:13:54,690
So JEPA stands for Joint
Embedding Predictive

1500
01:13:54,690 --> 01:13:55,770
Architecture.

1501
01:13:55,770 --> 01:14:00,050
So instead of predicting raw
outputs like the next token,

1502
01:14:00,050 --> 01:14:02,930
it instead predicts
latent representations.

1503
01:14:02,930 --> 01:14:05,730
So this focuses on latent
structure rather than

1504
01:14:05,730 --> 01:14:09,530
explicit surface details
like tokens themselves.

1505
01:14:09,530 --> 01:14:13,730
And this avoids the
modeling of low level noise,

1506
01:14:13,730 --> 01:14:16,970
but actually looks at
the latent structure.

1507
01:14:16,970 --> 01:14:20,090
So this leads to more data
efficient learning, better

1508
01:14:20,090 --> 01:14:24,850
abstraction and generalization,
and is arguably closer

1509
01:14:24,850 --> 01:14:27,610
to how humans learn, which
is through predicting

1510
01:14:27,610 --> 01:14:29,570
structure and
states of the world

1511
01:14:29,570 --> 01:14:33,570
rather than simply
pixels or tokens.

1512
01:14:33,570 --> 01:14:36,330
Next, there's this
alternative architecture

1513
01:14:36,330 --> 01:14:38,210
being investigated
these days called

1514
01:14:38,210 --> 01:14:40,490
State Space Models or SSMs.

1515
01:14:40,490 --> 01:14:43,810
And instead of modeling
things like transformers

1516
01:14:43,810 --> 01:14:47,750
and instead model sequences
using continuous state updates,

1517
01:14:47,750 --> 01:14:50,390
maintaining a compressed
internal state over time.

1518
01:14:50,390 --> 01:14:52,810
So you'll see that this
is a bit analogous to how

1519
01:14:52,810 --> 01:14:55,130
Recurrent Neural
Networks or RNNs actually

1520
01:14:55,130 --> 01:14:57,050
work through a hidden state.

1521
01:14:57,050 --> 01:14:59,550
And this leads to
more efficiency.

1522
01:14:59,550 --> 01:15:01,290
It's linear time
scaling rather than

1523
01:15:01,290 --> 01:15:03,930
quadratic with the number
of tokens like transformers

1524
01:15:03,930 --> 01:15:05,670
and the attention mechanism.

1525
01:15:05,670 --> 01:15:07,490
So it's more effective
for long sequences,

1526
01:15:07,490 --> 01:15:11,210
and it has stronger performance
on longer context tasks.

1527
01:15:11,210 --> 01:15:14,590
And a specific example of this
is the Mamba architecture,

1528
01:15:14,590 --> 01:15:16,910
which is a selective
state space model.

1529
01:15:16,910 --> 01:15:21,010
However, SSMs do
carry trade offs.

1530
01:15:21,010 --> 01:15:23,970
They're less flexible than
attention in certain settings.

1531
01:15:23,970 --> 01:15:25,970
And this is still
an earlier area

1532
01:15:25,970 --> 01:15:30,330
of research, which is
being investigated.

1533
01:15:30,330 --> 01:15:31,690
So thank you for listening.

1534
01:15:31,690 --> 01:15:33,090
That is our presentation.

1535
01:15:33,090 --> 01:15:37,290
So we touched upon how
transformers work, the evolution

1536
01:15:37,290 --> 01:15:43,830
of things from word embeddings,
Word2Vec to things like RNNs,

1537
01:15:43,830 --> 01:15:45,690
transformers,
pre-training as well

1538
01:15:45,690 --> 01:15:49,360
as post-training, various
post-training techniques,

1539
01:15:49,360 --> 01:15:52,240
remaining challenges and
weaknesses, things still

1540
01:15:52,240 --> 01:15:55,960
remaining between us and AGI
or superintelligence, things

1541
01:15:55,960 --> 01:15:59,340
like continual learning, proper
alignment, interpretability,

1542
01:15:59,340 --> 01:16:02,680
and potentially state space
models as well as world models

1543
01:16:02,680 --> 01:16:04,120
instead.

1544
01:16:04,120 --> 01:16:06,320
So starting next week,
we'll have speakers come in

1545
01:16:06,320 --> 01:16:08,820
to give interesting talks
about what they're working on.

1546
01:16:08,820 --> 01:16:12,180
So actually, next week,
we'll have Hazel Nam,

1547
01:16:12,180 --> 01:16:14,840
who will be coming to talk
about her work on JEPA and world

1548
01:16:14,840 --> 01:16:15,600
models.

1549
01:16:15,600 --> 01:16:18,800
And the week after we'll
be having Albert Goo

1550
01:16:18,800 --> 01:16:21,540
talk about his work on Mamba
and state space machines.

1551
01:16:21,540 --> 01:16:24,080
So, ironically, our
first two speakers

1552
01:16:24,080 --> 01:16:26,440
will not be talking about
transformers, but alternative

1553
01:16:26,440 --> 01:16:27,508
architectures.

1554
01:16:27,508 --> 01:16:29,800
But I highly encourage you
guys to learn more and think

1555
01:16:29,800 --> 01:16:31,280
